{"pattern_name": "Dynamic Module Import via Non-Literal String", "functionality": "The code dynamically imports a module using the `require` function, which is passed a non-literal string derived from a file object. This allows the application to load modules based on runtime data.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The vulnerability arises because `require` is called with a non-literal string (`file.full_name`). This allows an attacker to manipulate the input to load arbitrary files or modules, potentially leading to unintended code execution or information disclosure.", "vulnerable_example": "const moduleName = userInput; // userInput is controlled by an attacker\nrequire(moduleName);", "fixing_pattern": "To mitigate this vulnerability, ensure that `require` is only called with literal strings or validated, trusted inputs. Implement a whitelist of allowed module names or use a secure mapping mechanism to restrict which modules can be loaded.", "fixing_example": "const allowedModules = {\n    'module1': './modules/module1',\n    'module2': './modules/module2'\n};\nif (allowedModules[file.name]) {\n    require(allowedModules[file.name]);\n} else {\n    throw new Error('Invalid module name');\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Insufficient Validation of Service Request", "functionality": "The code processes a service request by checking if the requested service matches the expected service and then proceeds with the service acceptance logic.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code only checks if the requested service matches the expected service (`self._next_service`) but does not validate whether the request is coming from a client or server, nor does it verify if the key exchange (kex) is complete before processing the request.", "vulnerable_example": "if service == self._next_service:\n    self.logger.debug2('Accepting request for service %s', service)\n    self.send_packet(MSG_SERVICE_ACCEPT, String(service))\n    if (self.is_server() and \n            not self._auth_in_progress and\n            service == _USERAUTH_SERVICE):\n        self._auth_in_progress = True\n        self._send_deferred_packets()\nelse:\n    raise ServiceNotAvailable('Unexpected service request received')", "fixing_pattern": "The fix adds additional validation steps to ensure the request is not from a client and that the key exchange is complete before processing the service request. It also ensures the `_next_service` is reset after processing.", "fixing_example": "if self.is_client():\n    raise ProtocolError('Unexpected service request received')\nif not self._recv_encryption:\n    raise ProtocolError('Service request received before kex complete')\nif service != self._next_service:\n    raise ServiceNotAvailable('Unexpected service in service request')\nself.logger.debug2('Accepting request for service %s', service)\nself.send_packet(MSG_SERVICE_ACCEPT, String(service))\nself._next_service = None\nif service == _USERAUTH_SERVICE: \n    self._auth_in_progress = True\n    self._can_recv_ext_info = False\n    self._send_deferred_packets()"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure Environment Variable Access", "functionality": "The code allows access to environment variables through an API endpoint, which can be modified by users with write access.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Exposing environment variable access through an unprotected API endpoint, allowing potential modification of sensitive system configurations.", "vulnerable_example": "// Hypothetical vulnerable code\n@PostMapping(\"/env\")\npublic void updateEnv(@RequestBody Map<String, String> envUpdates) {\n    envUpdates.forEach((key, value) -> System.setenv(key, value));\n}", "fixing_pattern": "Restricting access to environment variable modification by either removing the endpoint or implementing proper access controls.", "fixing_example": "// Hypothetical fixed code\n@PostMapping(\"/env\")\n@PreAuthorize(\"hasRole('ADMIN')\")\npublic void updateEnv(@RequestBody Map<String, String> envUpdates) {\n    // Additional validation logic\n    envUpdates.forEach((key, value) -> System.setenv(key, value));\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Overly Complex Setup with Potential Security Risks", "functionality": "The code sets up a complex environment for testing, including mock objects and configurations for various components like identity, certificates, and DBus interfaces.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The setup method initializes multiple components and mocks, including DBus interfaces, without clear access control mechanisms. This complexity can lead to unintended exposure of sensitive methods or data.", "vulnerable_example": "def setUp(self):\n    self.addCleanup(patch.stopall)\n    cfg_patcher = patch.object(subscription_manager.managercli, 'cfg', new=stubs.config.CFG)\n    self.mock_cfg = cfg_patcher.start()\n    id_mock = NonCallableMock(name='FixtureIdentityMock')\n    id_mock.exists_and_valid = Mock(return_value=True)\n    id_mock.uuid = 'fixture_identity_mock_uuid'\n    id_mock.name = 'fixture_identity_mock_name'\n    id_mock.cert_dir_path = \"/not/a/real/path/to/pki/consumer/\"\n    id_mock.keypath.return_value = \"/not/a/real/key/path\"\n    id_mock.certpath.return_value = \"/not/a/real/cert/path\"\n    self.mock_calc = NonCallableMock()\n    self.mock_calc.calculate.return_value = None\n    self.mock_repofile_path_exists_patcher = patch('subscription_manager.repolib.RepoFile.path_exists')\n    mock_repofile_path_exists = self.mock_repofile_path_exists_patcher.start()\n    mock_repofile_path_exists.return_value = True\n    inj.provide(inj.IDENTITY, id_mock)\n    inj.provide(inj.PRODUCT_DATE_RANGE_CALCULATOR, self.mock_calc)\n    inj.provide(inj.ENTITLEMENT_STATUS_CACHE, stubs.StubEntitlementStatusCache())\n    inj.provide(inj.PROD_STATUS_CACHE, stubs.StubProductStatusCache())\n    inj.provide(inj.OVERRIDE_STATUS_CACHE, stubs.StubOverrideStatusCache())\n    inj.provide(inj.RELEASE_STATUS_CACHE, stubs.StubReleaseStatusCache())\n    inj.provide(inj.PROFILE_MANAGER, stubs.StubProfileManager())\n    self.ent_dir = stubs.StubEntitlementDirectory()\n    inj.provide(inj.ENT_DIR, self.ent_dir)\n    self.prod_dir = stubs.StubProductDirectory()\n    inj.provide(inj.PROD_DIR, self.prod_dir)\n    inj.provide(inj.INSTALLED_PRODUCTS_MANAGER, stubs.StubInstalledProductsManager())\n    self.stub_cp_provider = stubs.StubCPProvider()\n    self._release_versions = []\n    self.stub_cp_provider.content_connection.get_versions = self._get_release_versions\n    inj.provide(inj.CP_PROVIDER, self.stub_cp_provider)\n    inj.provide(inj.CERT_SORTER, stubs.StubCertSorter())\n    plugin_manager_mock = MagicMock(name='FixturePluginManagerMock')\n    plugin_manager_mock.runiter.return_value = iter([])\n    inj.provide(inj.PLUGIN_MANAGER, plugin_manager_mock)\n    inj.provide(inj.DBUS_IFACE, Mock(name='FixtureDbusIfaceMock'))\n    pooltype_cache = Mock()\n    inj.provide(inj.POOLTYPE_CACHE, pooltype_cache)\n    inj.provide(inj.ACTION_LOCK, RLock)\n    self.stub_facts = stubs.StubFacts()\n    inj.provide(inj.FACTS, self.stub_facts)\n    self.dbus_patcher = patch('subscription_manager.managercli.CliCommand._request_validity_check')\n    self.dbus_patcher.start()\n    self.is_valid_server_patcher = patch(\"subscription_manager.managercli.is_valid_server_info\")\n    is_valid_server_mock = self.is_valid_server_patcher.start()\n    is_valid_server_mock.return_value = True\n    self.test_proxy_connection_patcher = patch(\"subscription_manager.managercli.CliCommand.test_proxy_connection\")\n    test_proxy_connection_mock = self.test_proxy_connection_patcher.start()\n    test_proxy_connection_mock.return_value = True\n    self.files_to_cleanup = []", "fixing_pattern": "The fix simplifies the setup by removing the complex initialization of multiple components and mocks, reducing the attack surface and potential security risks.", "fixing_example": "def set_facts(self):\n    return {\"mock.facts\": \"true\"}"}
