{"pattern_name": "Insecure Static File Serving", "functionality": "The code serves static files without proper access control or path validation.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Serving static files based on user-supplied paths without proper validation or access control.", "vulnerable_example": "String filePath = request.getParameter(\"file\");\nFile file = new File(STATIC_DIR, filePath);\nresponse.sendFile(file);", "fixing_pattern": "Implement proper path validation and access control when serving static files.", "fixing_example": "String filePath = sanitizePath(request.getParameter(\"file\"));\nFile file = new File(STATIC_DIR, filePath).getCanonicalFile();\nif (!file.getCanonicalPath().startsWith(STATIC_DIR)) {\n    response.sendError(HttpServletResponse.SC_FORBIDDEN);\n    return;\n}\nresponse.sendFile(file);"}
{"pattern_name": "Overly Complex Setup with Potential Security Risks", "functionality": "The code sets up a complex environment for testing, including mock objects and configurations for various components like identity, certificates, and DBus interfaces.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The setup method initializes multiple components and mocks, including DBus interfaces, without clear access control mechanisms. This complexity can lead to unintended exposure of sensitive methods or data.", "vulnerable_example": "def setUp(self):\n    self.addCleanup(patch.stopall)\n    cfg_patcher = patch.object(subscription_manager.managercli, 'cfg', new=stubs.config.CFG)\n    self.mock_cfg = cfg_patcher.start()\n    id_mock = NonCallableMock(name='FixtureIdentityMock')\n    id_mock.exists_and_valid = Mock(return_value=True)\n    id_mock.uuid = 'fixture_identity_mock_uuid'\n    id_mock.name = 'fixture_identity_mock_name'\n    id_mock.cert_dir_path = \"/not/a/real/path/to/pki/consumer/\"\n    id_mock.keypath.return_value = \"/not/a/real/key/path\"\n    id_mock.certpath.return_value = \"/not/a/real/cert/path\"\n    self.mock_calc = NonCallableMock()\n    self.mock_calc.calculate.return_value = None\n    self.mock_repofile_path_exists_patcher = patch('subscription_manager.repolib.RepoFile.path_exists')\n    mock_repofile_path_exists = self.mock_repofile_path_exists_patcher.start()\n    mock_repofile_path_exists.return_value = True\n    inj.provide(inj.IDENTITY, id_mock)\n    inj.provide(inj.PRODUCT_DATE_RANGE_CALCULATOR, self.mock_calc)\n    inj.provide(inj.ENTITLEMENT_STATUS_CACHE, stubs.StubEntitlementStatusCache())\n    inj.provide(inj.PROD_STATUS_CACHE, stubs.StubProductStatusCache())\n    inj.provide(inj.OVERRIDE_STATUS_CACHE, stubs.StubOverrideStatusCache())\n    inj.provide(inj.RELEASE_STATUS_CACHE, stubs.StubReleaseStatusCache())\n    inj.provide(inj.PROFILE_MANAGER, stubs.StubProfileManager())\n    self.ent_dir = stubs.StubEntitlementDirectory()\n    inj.provide(inj.ENT_DIR, self.ent_dir)\n    self.prod_dir = stubs.StubProductDirectory()\n    inj.provide(inj.PROD_DIR, self.prod_dir)\n    inj.provide(inj.INSTALLED_PRODUCTS_MANAGER, stubs.StubInstalledProductsManager())\n    self.stub_cp_provider = stubs.StubCPProvider()\n    self._release_versions = []\n    self.stub_cp_provider.content_connection.get_versions = self._get_release_versions\n    inj.provide(inj.CP_PROVIDER, self.stub_cp_provider)\n    inj.provide(inj.CERT_SORTER, stubs.StubCertSorter())\n    plugin_manager_mock = MagicMock(name='FixturePluginManagerMock')\n    plugin_manager_mock.runiter.return_value = iter([])\n    inj.provide(inj.PLUGIN_MANAGER, plugin_manager_mock)\n    inj.provide(inj.DBUS_IFACE, Mock(name='FixtureDbusIfaceMock'))\n    pooltype_cache = Mock()\n    inj.provide(inj.POOLTYPE_CACHE, pooltype_cache)\n    inj.provide(inj.ACTION_LOCK, RLock)\n    self.stub_facts = stubs.StubFacts()\n    inj.provide(inj.FACTS, self.stub_facts)\n    self.dbus_patcher = patch('subscription_manager.managercli.CliCommand._request_validity_check')\n    self.dbus_patcher.start()\n    self.is_valid_server_patcher = patch(\"subscription_manager.managercli.is_valid_server_info\")\n    is_valid_server_mock = self.is_valid_server_patcher.start()\n    is_valid_server_mock.return_value = True\n    self.test_proxy_connection_patcher = patch(\"subscription_manager.managercli.CliCommand.test_proxy_connection\")\n    test_proxy_connection_mock = self.test_proxy_connection_patcher.start()\n    test_proxy_connection_mock.return_value = True\n    self.files_to_cleanup = []", "fixing_pattern": "The fix simplifies the setup by removing the complex initialization of multiple components and mocks, reducing the attack surface and potential security risks.", "fixing_example": "def set_facts(self):\n    return {\"mock.facts\": \"true\"}"}
{"pattern_name": "Hardcoded Credentials for Non-Existent User", "functionality": "The code tests login functionality by simulating a login attempt with a non-existent email address and verifying the response message.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code uses hardcoded credentials ('anypassword') for a non-existent email address, which is an unsafe practice. Hardcoded credentials can be exploited if the test code is deployed or exposed.", "vulnerable_example": "def test_login_with_nonexistent_email(testapp):\n    res = testapp.get('/')\n    form = res.forms['loginForm']\n    form['email'] = 'fakeuser@example.com'\n    form['password'] = 'testpassword456'\n    res = form.submit()\n    assert 'Invalid login credentials' in res.text", "fixing_pattern": "Replace hardcoded credentials with dynamically generated or securely managed credentials. Use environment variables or a secret management system to store and retrieve test credentials securely.", "fixing_example": "import os\n\ndef test_login_with_nonexistent_email(testapp):\n    res = testapp.get('/')\n    form = res.forms['loginForm']\n    form['email'] = 'fakeuser@example.com'\n    form['password'] = os.getenv('TEST_NONEXISTENT_PASSWORD')\n    res = form.submit()\n    assert 'Invalid login credentials' in res.text"}
{"pattern_name": "Missing Authorization Flag Initialization", "functionality": "The code initializes various configuration parameters for a system but lacks proper initialization of an authorization-related flag.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Failure to initialize or set an authorization control flag during object initialization, potentially leading to unauthorized access or actions.", "vulnerable_example": "class Config:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.type = None\n        self.quiet = None\n        # Missing authorization control flag initialization", "fixing_pattern": "Adding proper initialization of an authorization control flag to ensure proper access control is enforced by default.", "fixing_example": "class Config:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.type = None\n        self.quiet = None\n        self.with_other_user = True  # Added authorization control flag"}
{"pattern_name": "Unsafe Socket Address Initialization", "functionality": "The code initializes a socket address with a default port of 0, which may expose residual data or unintended behavior.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The socket address is initialized with a port of 0, which is a reserved port and may lead to unintended behavior or exposure of residual data.", "vulnerable_example": "public ConfigurationUpdater() {\n    this.socketAddress = new InetSocketAddress(0);\n}", "fixing_pattern": "The socket address should be initialized with a valid, non-reserved port or left uninitialized until a valid host and port are provided.", "fixing_example": "public ConfigurationUpdater() {\n    this.socketAddress = null; // Leave uninitialized until valid host and port are provided\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure Session Storage Configuration", "functionality": "The code initializes a Flask application but does not properly configure session storage, potentially leading to insecure session handling.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Missing explicit session storage configuration, which could default to insecure implementations or behaviors.", "vulnerable_example": "Initialization of Flask app without session configuration:\n\nself.flask_app = Flask(name, static_folder=assets_folder, static_url_path=\"/\")", "fixing_pattern": "Explicitly configuring session storage to use a secure filesystem-based session storage mechanism.", "fixing_example": "Adding secure session configuration:\n\nself.flask_app.config['SESSION_TYPE'] = \"filesystem\"\nSession(self.flask_app)"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure Localhost Check", "functionality": "The code checks if a request originates from localhost by verifying the client's IP address.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using IP address to verify if the request is from localhost without proper validation of the IP source.", "vulnerable_example": "String clientIp = request.getHeader(\"X-Forwarded-For\");\nif (clientIp == null) clientIp = request.getRemoteAddr();\nif (!InetAddress.getByName(clientIp).isLoopbackAddress()) {\n    response.sendError(HttpServletResponse.SC_FORBIDDEN,\n            \"Git hook callbacks can only be accessed from localhost.\");\n    return;\n}", "fixing_pattern": "Replacing the IP-based localhost check with a token-based authentication mechanism to ensure the request is from a trusted source.", "fixing_example": "List<String> fields = StringUtils.splitAndTrim(request.getPathInfo(), \"/\");\nPreconditions.checkState(fields.size() == 3);\nif (!fields.get(2).equals(GitUtils.HOOK_TOKEN)) {\n    response.sendError(HttpServletResponse.SC_FORBIDDEN,\n            \"Git hook callbacks can only be accessed by OneDev itself\");\n    return;\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Missing Resource Cleanup on Error Path", "functionality": "The code initializes resources (e.g., I/O space) but fails to clean them up properly if an error occurs during initialization.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The error handling path does not include cleanup of previously allocated resources, leading to potential resource leaks or use-after-free vulnerabilities.", "vulnerable_example": "out_err:\n  kfree(init_name);\n  return rv;", "fixing_pattern": "The fix adds cleanup of I/O resources in the error handling path to ensure that all allocated resources are properly released before returning.", "fixing_example": "out_err:\n  if (rv && new_smi->io.io_cleanup) {\n    new_smi->io.io_cleanup(&new_smi->io);\n    new_smi->io.io_cleanup = NULL;\n  }\n  kfree(init_name);\n  return rv;"}
{"pattern_name": "Endpoint Links Resolution", "functionality": "The code resolves endpoint links by passing a collection of endpoints to a resolver, which is used to generate links for the endpoints.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Directly passing the collection of endpoints to the resolver without ensuring proper security checks or validation.", "vulnerable_example": "new EndpointLinksResolver(allEndpoints)", "fixing_pattern": "Removing the explicit use of EndpointLinksResolver and directly passing the collection of endpoints to the handler mapping, which may include internal security checks.", "fixing_example": "allEndpoints"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure Client Type Initialization", "functionality": "Creating a client application with insufficient security restrictions during initialization", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using a generic client creation method that doesn't enforce proper security levels", "vulnerable_example": "client = KeycloakModelUtils.createClient(realm, Constants.BROKER_SERVICE_CLIENT_ID);", "fixing_pattern": "Using a specialized client creation method that enforces proper security levels and management restrictions", "fixing_example": "client = KeycloakModelUtils.createManagementClient(realm, Constants.BROKER_SERVICE_CLIENT_ID);"}
{"pattern_name": "Unnecessary Resource Initialization on Connection", "functionality": "The code initializes backend plugins and prepares them for each new connection, even if the connection might not proceed to the actual request processing phase.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code performs backend initialization (`backend_open` and `backend_prepare`) immediately after establishing a connection, regardless of whether the connection will be used for actual requests.", "vulnerable_example": "lock_request (conn);\nr = backend_open (backend, conn, read_only);\nunlock_request (conn);\nif (r == -1)\n  goto done;\nlock_request (conn);\nif (backend)\n  r = backend_prepare (backend, conn);\nelse\n  r = 0;\nunlock_request (conn);\nif (r == -1)\n  goto done;", "fixing_pattern": "The fix removes the unnecessary backend initialization (`backend_open` and `backend_prepare`) steps during the connection phase, deferring them until they are actually needed for processing requests.", "fixing_example": ""}
{"pattern_name": "Unhandled Certificate Exceptions", "functionality": "The code handles SSL/TLS certificates for secure connections.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Catching only specific certificate exceptions, potentially missing other critical SSL/TLS errors.", "vulnerable_example": "except X509.X509Error, e:", "fixing_pattern": "Broadening the exception handling to catch all relevant SSL/TLS errors, ensuring comprehensive error management.", "fixing_example": "except (CertificateException, ssl.SSLError) as e:"}
{"pattern_name": "Arbitrary Code Execution via Dynamic Module Import", "functionality": "The code dynamically imports a module using the `require` function with a non-literal string, which is derived from user input or external sources.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using `require` with a non-literal string allows an attacker to manipulate the input to load arbitrary files or modules, potentially leading to arbitrary code execution if the attacker has write access to the file system.", "vulnerable_example": "const modulePath = userInput; // userInput is untrusted\nconst module = require(modulePath);", "fixing_pattern": "Avoid using `require` with dynamic paths. Instead, use safer methods to load and parse configuration files, such as `fs.readFileSync` combined with `JSON.parse`. Additionally, validate and sanitize the input to ensure it points to a safe and expected location.", "fixing_example": "const modulePath = sanitizePath(userInput); // sanitizePath ensures the path is safe\nconst moduleContent = fs.readFileSync(modulePath, 'utf8');\nconst module = JSON.parse(moduleContent);"}
{"pattern_name": "Unvalidated Server Information", "functionality": "The code processes server information such as hostname, port, and prefix for establishing connections.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Lack of validation for server information before using it to establish connections.", "vulnerable_example": "cfg.set(\"server\", \"hostname\", self.server_hostname)", "fixing_pattern": "Adding validation checks for server information to ensure it is safe and correct before use.", "fixing_example": "conf[\"server\"][\"hostname\"] = self.server_hostname"}
{"pattern_name": "Missing Network Device Flag Initialization", "functionality": "The code initializes a network device structure but fails to properly configure certain flags that control packet handling behavior.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code does not clear the IFF_TX_SKB_SHARING flag, which could lead to improper handling of shared socket buffers.", "vulnerable_example": "static void _isdn_setup(struct net_device *dev)\n{\n    isdn_net_local *lp = netdev_priv(dev);\n    ether_setup(dev);\n    dev->flags = IFF_NOARP|IFF_POINTOPOINT;\n    // Missing dev->priv_flags &= ~IFF_TX_SKB_SHARING;\n    ...\n}", "fixing_pattern": "The fix adds the clearing of the IFF_TX_SKB_SHARING flag to prevent shared socket buffer handling issues.", "fixing_example": "static void _isdn_setup(struct net_device *dev)\n{\n    isdn_net_local *lp = netdev_priv(dev);\n    ether_setup(dev);\n    dev->flags = IFF_NOARP|IFF_POINTOPOINT;\n    dev->priv_flags &= ~IFF_TX_SKB_SHARING;\n    ...\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Incorrect Module Definition Initialization", "functionality": "The code initializes a Python module definition structure for the AST parser, which is used to parse Python source code into an abstract syntax tree.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using an incorrect module definition structure name (`_astmodule3`) that may lead to undefined behavior or crashes when the module is initialized.", "vulnerable_example": "m = PyModule_Create(&_astmodule3);", "fixing_pattern": "Correcting the module definition structure name to match the intended module definition (`_astmodule`).", "fixing_example": "m = PyModule_Create(&_astmodule);"}
{"pattern_name": "Dynamic Module Import with Non-Literal String", "functionality": "The code dynamically imports various modules using the `require` function to perform tasks such as compiling Sass files, TypeScript files, HTML templates, concatenating JavaScript files, and cleaning the build directory.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The vulnerability arises from using a non-literal string (constructed from `DEPS_FOLDER`) in the `require` function. This allows an attacker to manipulate the module path, potentially leading to the execution of arbitrary code or reading arbitrary files if they have write access to the file system.", "vulnerable_example": "var module = require(DEPS_FOLDER + 'another-module');", "fixing_pattern": "To fix this vulnerability, use literal strings for module imports or validate and sanitize the `DEPS_FOLDER` variable to ensure it cannot be manipulated. Alternatively, use a whitelist of allowed modules to prevent unauthorized imports.", "fixing_example": "var module = require('path/to/another-module');"}
{"pattern_name": "Insufficient Policy Rights Check", "functionality": "The code checks if a module is authorized to be registered based on the policy rights defined in the system. It uses a policy domain and rights to determine if the module can be registered.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code checks for `ReadPolicyRights` instead of `AllPolicyRights`, which may allow unauthorized modules to be registered if they only require read access.", "vulnerable_example": "rights=ReadPolicyRights;", "fixing_pattern": "The fix changes the rights check from `ReadPolicyRights` to `AllPolicyRights`, ensuring that all policy rights are considered when authorizing a module.", "fixing_example": "rights=AllPolicyRights;"}
{"pattern_name": "Insufficient Input Validation in System Control Handler", "functionality": "The code handles system control table entries by processing user-provided input values without proper validation.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using a generic input processing function (proc_dointvec) that does not enforce value constraints, allowing potentially dangerous values to be set.", "vulnerable_example": "int ret = proc_dointvec(table, write, buffer, lenp, ppos);", "fixing_pattern": "Replacing the generic input processing function with a constrained version (proc_dointvec_minmax) that enforces minimum and maximum value limits.", "fixing_example": "int ret = proc_dointvec_minmax(table, write, buffer, lenp, ppos);"}
{"pattern_name": "Unsafe File Path Handling with Flask's send_file", "functionality": "The code uses Flask's send_file function to serve files to the client. The file path is constructed using user input without proper validation or sanitization.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using user-controlled input directly in Flask's send_file function without validating or sanitizing the file path, allowing for absolute path traversal.", "vulnerable_example": "from flask import Flask, send_file\n\napp = Flask(__name__)\n\n@app.route('/file/<path:filename>')\ndef download_file(filename):\n    return send_file(filename)", "fixing_pattern": "Validate and sanitize the user input to ensure the file path is within the intended directory, preventing path traversal attacks.", "fixing_example": "from flask import Flask, send_file, abort\nimport os\n\napp = Flask(__name__)\n\n@app.route('/file/<path:filename>')\ndef download_file(filename):\n    base_dir = '/safe/directory'\n    file_path = os.path.join(base_dir, filename)\n    if not os.path.abspath(file_path).startswith(base_dir):\n        abort(404)\n    return send_file(file_path)"}
{"pattern_name": "Unrestricted Application Property Routing", "functionality": "The code uses application properties to determine the routing expression for function execution. These properties can be used to dynamically construct URLs or other resources.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The routing expression from application properties is passed directly to a function without validation or restriction, allowing potential manipulation of the expression to bypass URL restrictions.", "vulnerable_example": "function = this.functionFromExpression(functionProperties.getRoutingExpression(), message);", "fixing_pattern": "The routing expression from application properties is now passed with an additional boolean parameter to enforce validation or restrictions, preventing unauthorized URL access.", "fixing_example": "function = this.functionFromExpression(functionProperties.getRoutingExpression(), message, false);"}
{"pattern_name": "Unvalidated Redirect in CSRF-Prone Endpoint", "functionality": "The code handles a POST request to accept terms and conditions, including a redirect URL parameter that determines where the user is sent after acceptance.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code accepts and uses a user-controlled redirect URL ('returnTo') without proper validation or sanitization, making it vulnerable to CSRF attacks where an attacker could manipulate the redirect destination.", "vulnerable_example": "self.client.post(\"/terms/accept/\", {\"terms\": 1, \"returnTo\": \"/secure/\"}, follow=True)", "fixing_pattern": "The fix abstracts the redirect URL into a parameter that is controlled by the server-side logic rather than being directly passed from the client. This ensures that the redirect destination is validated and safe.", "fixing_example": "self.client.post(\"/terms/accept/\", {\"terms\": 1, \"returnTo\": return_to}, follow=True)"}
{"pattern_name": "Information Leakage via Error Messages", "functionality": "The code checks if a user exists by attempting to reset their password and then returns the user's email address in the response.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code exposes sensitive information (user email) in the response, which can be used to determine if a user exists in the system.", "vulnerable_example": "when(requestResponse.getUserEmail()).thenReturn(userEmail);\nassertEquals(userEmail, this.scriptService.requestResetPassword(userReference));", "fixing_pattern": "The fix removes the exposure of sensitive information by not returning the user's email in the response, thus preventing information leakage.", "fixing_example": "this.scriptService.requestResetPassword(userReference);"}
{"pattern_name": "Unsanitized Path Injection", "functionality": "The code creates a Windows service using a file path provided as input without proper sanitization.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using user-provided input directly in system calls without proper sanitization or escaping, which can lead to unexpected behavior or exploitation.", "vulnerable_example": "WindowsCoreFunctions::toConstWCharArray( filePath )", "fixing_pattern": "Sanitizing the input by removing potentially dangerous characters (like quotes) and properly formatting the path before using it in system calls.", "fixing_example": "const auto binaryPath = QStringLiteral(\"\\\"%1\\\"\").arg( QString( filePath ).replace( QLatin1Char('\"'), QString() ) );\nWindowsCoreFunctions::toConstWCharArray( binaryPath )"}
{"pattern_name": "Uncontrolled Resource Consumption", "functionality": "The code handles the deletion of monitored items in a subscription but fails to properly manage the count of monitored items, leading to potential resource exhaustion.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code does not decrement the monitored item count when items are deleted, which can lead to an incorrect count and potential resource exhaustion.", "vulnerable_example": "deleteResults[i] = StatusCode.GOOD;", "fixing_pattern": "The fix involves decrementing the monitored item count when items are deleted to ensure accurate tracking and prevent resource exhaustion.", "fixing_example": "deleteResults[i] = StatusCode.GOOD;\nmonitoredItemCount.decrementAndGet();\nserver.getMonitoredItemCount().decrementAndGet();"}
{"pattern_name": "Unnecessary Curly Braces in Control Flow", "functionality": "The code checks conditions and returns a boolean value based on the evaluation of those conditions.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using unnecessary curly braces for single-statement if/for blocks, which can reduce code readability.", "vulnerable_example": "if (nbArg > args.size()) {\n    return false;\n}", "fixing_pattern": "Removing unnecessary curly braces for single-statement if/for blocks to improve code readability and conciseness.", "fixing_example": "if (nbArg > args.size())\n    return false;"}
{"pattern_name": "Missing Initialization of Critical Component", "functionality": "The code sets up mock objects and configurations for testing but fails to initialize a critical component required for proper execution.", "vulnerable_pattern": "A critical component (BlockAsyncRendererExecutor) is not initialized during the test setup, which could lead to incomplete or incorrect test behavior.", "vulnerable_example": "@BeforeEach\npublic void beforeEach() throws Exception\n{\n    BeanDescriptor descriptor = mock(BeanDescriptor.class);\n    when(descriptor.getProperties()).thenReturn(Collections.emptyList());\n    when(this.beanManager.getBeanDescriptor(any())).thenReturn(descriptor);\n    when(this.dab.getCurrentAuthorReference()).thenReturn(AUTHOR);\n    when(this.macroReferenceResolver.resolve(eq(\"target\"), any())).thenReturn(TARGET_REFERENCE);\n    when(this.resolver.resolve(\"source\")).thenReturn(SOURCE_REFERENCE);\n}", "fixing_pattern": "The fix involves initializing the missing critical component (BlockAsyncRendererExecutor) during the test setup to ensure proper execution and test coverage.", "fixing_example": "@BeforeEach\npublic void beforeEach() throws Exception\n{\n    BeanDescriptor descriptor = mock(BeanDescriptor.class);\n    when(descriptor.getProperties()).thenReturn(Collections.emptyList());\n    when(this.beanManager.getBeanDescriptor(any())).thenReturn(descriptor);\n    when(this.dab.getCurrentAuthorReference()).thenReturn(AUTHOR);\n    when(this.macroReferenceResolver.resolve(eq(\"target\"), any())).thenReturn(TARGET_REFERENCE);\n    when(this.resolver.resolve(\"source\")).thenReturn(SOURCE_REFERENCE);\n    this.executor = this.componentManager.getInstance(BlockAsyncRendererExecutor.class);\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Information Leak via Error Message", "functionality": "The code checks if a user exists and returns a response containing sensitive information (email) in the verification process.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Returning sensitive information (email) in the response object, which can be used to infer the existence of a user account.", "vulnerable_example": "new DefaultResetPasswordRequestResponse(this.userReference, email, newVerificationCode);", "fixing_pattern": "Removing sensitive information (email) from the response object to prevent information leakage.", "fixing_example": "new DefaultResetPasswordRequestResponse(this.userReference, newVerificationCode);"}
{"pattern_name": "Missing Null Check Before Dereference", "functionality": "The code processes a packet or response without validating if the payload or critical components are null.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Accessing or processing data from a potentially null object without proper null checks.", "vulnerable_example": "// Vulnerable code that processes a response without checking for null\nOAuthClient.AccessTokenResponse response = oauth.doGrantAccessTokenRequest(\"secret\", \"\", \"\", null);\nassertEquals(401, response.getStatusCode());", "fixing_pattern": "Adding validation to ensure that the object is not null before accessing its properties or methods.", "fixing_example": "// Fixed code with proper validation\nOAuthClient.AccessTokenResponse response = oauth.doGrantAccessTokenRequest(\"secret\", \"\", \"\", null);\nif (response != null) {\n    assertEquals(200, response.getStatusCode());\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Resource Leak on Error Path", "functionality": "The code attempts to bind ports and start a connection. If either operation fails, it enters a retry loop with an increasing delay.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code does not properly clean up resources (unbind ports) before entering the retry loop when an error occurs.", "vulnerable_example": "if(bind_ports() | exec_connect_start()) {\n    s_poll_sleep(delay/1000, delay%1000);\n    signal_post(SIGNAL_RELOAD_CONFIG);\n    delay*=2;\n    if(delay > 10000)\n        delay=10000;\n} else {\n    delay=10;\n}", "fixing_pattern": "Added resource cleanup (unbind_ports) before entering the retry loop to ensure proper resource management on error paths.", "fixing_example": "if(bind_ports() | exec_connect_start()) {\n    unbind_ports();\n    s_poll_sleep(delay/1000, delay%1000);\n    signal_post(SIGNAL_RELOAD_CONFIG);\n    delay*=2;\n    if(delay > 10000)\n        delay=10000;\n} else {\n    delay=10;\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Incorrect Process Execution Check", "functionality": "The code attempts to check if a specific executable (e.g., Docker) is installed by attempting to start a process with the executable.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using process execution to check for the existence of an executable, which can lead to unnecessary resource usage, potential security risks, or incorrect results if the process fails for reasons other than the executable not being present.", "vulnerable_example": "try {\n  new ProcessBuilder(dockerExecutable.toString()).start();\n  return true;\n} catch (IOException ex) {\n  return false;\n}", "fixing_pattern": "Replacing the process execution check with a simpler and safer file existence check to verify the presence of the executable.", "fixing_example": "return Files.exists(dockerExecutable);"}
{"pattern_name": "Insufficient Access Control for Localhost Verification", "functionality": "The code verifies if a request is coming from localhost by checking the client's IP address. This is used to restrict access to certain functionalities.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code relies on the 'X-Forwarded-For' header and the remote address to determine if the request is from localhost. This can be spoofed or bypassed if the header is manipulated.", "vulnerable_example": "String clientIp = request.getHeader(\"X-Forwarded-For\");\nif (clientIp == null) clientIp = request.getRemoteAddr();\nif (!InetAddress.getByName(clientIp).isLoopbackAddress()) {\n    response.sendError(HttpServletResponse.SC_FORBIDDEN, \"Git hook callbacks can only be accessed from localhost.\");\n    return;\n}", "fixing_pattern": "The code now verifies access by checking a token in the request path, ensuring that only OneDev itself can access the functionality. This removes the reliance on IP-based verification.", "fixing_example": "List<String> fields = StringUtils.splitAndTrim(request.getPathInfo(), \"/\");\nPreconditions.checkState(fields.size() == 3);\nif (!fields.get(2).equals(GitUtils.HOOK_TOKEN)) {\n    response.sendError(HttpServletResponse.SC_FORBIDDEN, \"Git hook callbacks can only be accessed by OneDev itself\");\n    return;\n}"}
