{"pattern_name": "Missing CSRF Protection in HTTP POST Handler", "functionality": "The code handles user registration by accepting a POST request with user registration data, validating the input, checking for existing users, and adding a new user to the repository.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The HTTP POST handler lacks the `[ValidateAntiForgeryToken]` attribute, making it vulnerable to Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) attacks. Without this attribute, the application cannot verify that the request originated from an authenticated user, allowing malicious actors to forge requests and perform unauthorized actions.", "vulnerable_example": "[HttpPost]\npublic IActionResult UpdateProfile([FromBody] UserProfileModel profile)\n{\n    if (profile == null)\n    {\n        return BadRequest(\"Invalid profile data.\");\n    }\n\n    userRepository.UpdateProfile(profile);\n    return Ok(\"Profile updated successfully.\");\n}", "fixing_pattern": "To mitigate CSRF vulnerabilities, the `[ValidateAntiForgeryToken]` attribute should be added to the HTTP POST handler. This ensures that the request includes a valid anti-forgery token, verifying its authenticity and preventing unauthorized requests.", "fixing_example": "[HttpPost]\n[ValidateAntiForgeryToken]\npublic IActionResult RegisterUser([FromBody] UserRegistrationModel userRegistration)\n{\n    if (userRegistration == null)\n    {\n        return BadRequest(\"Invalid user registration data.\");\n    }\n\n    if (!ModelState.IsValid)\n    {\n        return BadRequest(ModelState);\n    }\n\n    if (userRepository.UserExists(userRegistration.Username))\n    {\n        return BadRequest(\"Username already exists.\");\n    }\n\n    if (!userRepository.IsEmailValid(userRegistration.Email))\n    {\n        return BadRequest(\"Invalid email address.\");\n    }\n\n    var user = new User\n    {\n        Username = userRegistration.Username,\n        Email = userRegistration.Email,\n        PasswordHash = HashPassword(userRegistration.Password)\n    };\n\n    userRepository.AddUser(user);\n    return Ok(\"User registered successfully.\");\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Insufficient Session Expiration", "functionality": "The code handles user login and session management, including creating new users and updating user attributes.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code uses `add()` method to save user objects, which may not properly handle session expiration or persistence, leading to potential session management issues.", "vulnerable_example": "userobj.add()", "fixing_pattern": "The code is updated to use `commit()` method instead of `add()` to ensure proper session management and persistence, which helps in enforcing session expiration policies.", "fixing_example": "userobj.commit()"}
{"pattern_name": "Unsafe Exception Handling in Token Generation", "functionality": "The code generates a JWT token for a user with an expiration time and logs the user's successful login.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Catching a generic Exception and logging the error message without proper handling, then rethrowing a custom exception.", "vulnerable_example": "try {\n  instance.add(Calendar.HOUR, 24);\n  String token =\n      JWT.create()\n          .withClaim(\"userId\", user.getId())\n          .withClaim(\"name\", user.getName())\n          .withExpiresAt(instance.getTime())\n          .sign(Algorithm.HMAC256(\"IOTDB:\" + InetAddress.getLocalHost().getHostAddress()));\n  logger.info(user.getName() + \"login successfully\");\n  return token;\n} catch (Exception e) {\n  logger.info(e.getMessage());\n  throw new BaseException(ErrorCode.GET_TOKEN_FAIL, ErrorCode.GET_TOKEN_FAIL_MSG);\n}", "fixing_pattern": "The code was completely replaced with a simple HTML response for a welcome page, removing the token generation logic entirely.", "fixing_example": "@GetMapping(\"/\")\npublic String welcome() {\n  String str =\n      \"<!DOCTYPE html>\\n\"\n          + \"<html lang=\\\"ch\\\">\\n\"\n          + \"<head>\\n\"\n          + \"    <meta charset=\\\"UTF-8\\\">\\n\"\n          + \"    <title>welcome</title>\\n\"\n          + \"</head>\\n\"\n          + \"<body>\\n\"\n          + \"<h1>You have successfully started IoTDB-Workbench backend application!</h1>\\n\"\n          + \"<h2>For a better experience with IOTDB-Workbench, Please refer to the <a href=\\\"https://github.com/loveher147/iotdb-admin/blob/main/backend/doc/deploy.md\\\">deployment documentation</a> for deployment</h2>\\n\"\n          + \"</body>\\n\"\n          + \"</html>\";\n  return str;\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure URL Path Construction", "functionality": "The code constructs a URL path by combining a base path prefix with a user-provided path, potentially allowing path manipulation.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code blindly accepts and processes user-provided path without proper validation or sanitization, potentially allowing path traversal or manipulation.", "vulnerable_example": "def _build_url(self, path, queries=()):\n    if not path.startswith(self.path_prefix):\n        if path.startswith('/'):\n            path = path[1:]\n        path = '/'.join((self.path_prefix, path))", "fixing_pattern": "Added a parameter to explicitly control whether the path prefix should be ignored, allowing for more secure handling of path construction.", "fixing_example": "def _build_url(self, path, queries, ignore_prefix):\n    if not path.startswith(self.path_prefix) and not ignore_prefix:\n        if path.startswith('/'):\n            path = path[1:]\n        path = '/'.join((self.path_prefix, path))"}
{"pattern_name": "Open Redirect Vulnerability", "functionality": "The code handles user registration by validating input, creating a registration request, and processing it through a service. Upon successful registration, it redirects the user to a specified URL or a default page.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code directly uses user-supplied input (`returnUrl`) in a redirect operation without validating whether the URL is safe or belongs to the application's domain. This allows attackers to craft malicious URLs that redirect users to external, potentially harmful sites.", "vulnerable_example": "public ActionResult Login(string returnUrl)\n{\n    if (User.Identity.IsAuthenticated)\n    {\n        return Redirect(returnUrl);\n    }\n    return View();\n}", "fixing_pattern": "To prevent open redirects, the application should validate that the `returnUrl` is a local URL (i.e., it belongs to the application's domain) before performing the redirect. This can be achieved using built-in methods like `Url.IsLocalUrl()` or custom validation logic.", "fixing_example": "public ActionResult Register(UserRegistrationModel model, string returnUrl)\n{\n    if (ModelState.IsValid)\n    {\n        var registerRequest = new UserRegistrationRequest(\n            model.User, \n            model.Email, \n            model.Mobile, \n            model.UserName, \n            model.Password, \n            PasswordFormat.Encrypted, \n            model.IsApprove\n        );\n\n        var registrationResult = _accountService.RegisterUser(registerRequest);\n\n        if (registrationResult.Success)\n        {\n            if (model.IsApprove)\n            {\n                AuthenticationService.SignIn(model.User, true);\n            }\n\n            if (String.IsNullOrEmpty(returnUrl) || !Url.IsLocalUrl(returnUrl))\n            {\n                return RedirectToAction(\"Index\", \"Home\");\n            }\n\n            return Redirect(returnUrl);\n        }\n        else\n        {\n            foreach (var error in registrationResult.Errors)\n            {\n                ModelState.AddModelError(\"\", error);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    return View(model);\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure JWT Token Generation with Hardcoded Secret", "functionality": "The code generates a JWT token for a user, including user-specific claims and a hardcoded secret for signing the token.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using a hardcoded secret key for signing JWT tokens, which can be easily exploited if the secret is discovered.", "vulnerable_example": "return Jwts.builder()\n        .setHeaderParam(\"type\", \"JWT\")\n        .setSubject(user.getId() + \"\")\n        .setIssuedAt(now)\n        .claim(\"userId\", user.getId())\n        .claim(\"name\", user.getName())\n        .setExpiration(expireDate)\n        .signWith(SignatureAlgorithm.HS512, secret)\n        .compact();", "fixing_pattern": "Removing the insecure token generation method and replacing it with a method to securely set the secret key externally, allowing for better control and management of the secret.", "fixing_example": "public void setSecret(String payload) {\n    secret = payload;\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Sensitive Information Exposure in Error Handling", "functionality": "The code handles authentication and logs error messages when authentication fails or when the endpoint is misconfigured.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Logging sensitive information (username and password) in cleartext when authentication fails or when the endpoint is misconfigured.", "vulnerable_example": "The original code does not explicitly show the logging of sensitive information, but the vulnerability description indicates that the Swift endpoint's username and password were logged in cleartext in error messages when the endpoint was misconfigured or unusable.", "fixing_pattern": "Remove or obfuscate sensitive information from error messages and logs. Ensure that sensitive data is not exposed in error handling or logging mechanisms.", "fixing_example": "The commit does not directly show the fix for this vulnerability, but the general fix would involve modifying the error handling code to ensure that sensitive information is not logged. For example:\n\n```python\ntry:\n    # authentication logic\n    pass\nexcept Exception as e:\n    logger.error('Authentication failed: %s', str(e))\n    # Do not include sensitive information in the error message\n```"}
{"pattern_name": "Hardcoded Credentials in Test Code", "functionality": "The code tests the login functionality of a web application by sending HTTP POST requests with both invalid and valid credentials to a login endpoint. It then checks the response for expected error messages or successful login indicators.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code hardcodes sensitive credentials (usernames and passwords) directly in the source code. This practice is insecure because it exposes credentials to anyone with access to the codebase, making it easier for attackers to exploit them.", "vulnerable_example": "def test_login_functionality():\n    username = 'admin'\n    password = 'admin123'\n    response = requests.post('https://example.com/login', data={'username': username, 'password': password})", "fixing_pattern": "To resolve this issue, credentials should be stored securely using a secrets management system or environment variables. This ensures that sensitive information is not embedded in the code and can be managed securely.", "fixing_example": "import os\n\ndef test_login_functionality():\n    username = os.getenv('TEST_USERNAME')\n    password = os.getenv('TEST_PASSWORD')\n    response = requests.post('https://example.com/login', data={'username': username, 'password': password})"}
{"pattern_name": "Incorrect Request Scope Isolation", "functionality": "The code handles HTTP requests in an ASGI environment but fails to properly isolate request scopes, potentially allowing data leakage between requests.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using a shared resource or client instance across different request scopes without proper isolation mechanisms.", "vulnerable_example": "resource_for_federation=Mock(), http_client=None, config=config", "fixing_pattern": "Introducing a dedicated client instance for specific functionality to ensure proper request scope isolation.", "fixing_example": "resource_for_federation=Mock(), federation_http_client=None, config=config"}
{"pattern_name": "Unsafe HTML Rendering with `mark_safe`", "functionality": "The code constructs an HTML message containing user-controlled data and marks it as safe for rendering in the browser, bypassing Django's default HTML escaping.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using `mark_safe()` to render user-controlled data directly into HTML without proper escaping, which can lead to XSS vulnerabilities.", "vulnerable_example": "messages.success(request, mark_safe(msg))", "fixing_pattern": "Replacing `mark_safe()` with `format_html()` to safely render HTML content by automatically escaping user-controlled data.", "fixing_example": "messages.success(request, msg)"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure Temporary File Creation", "functionality": "The code downloads and executes a script from a remote server in a temporary directory.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code uses a hardcoded temporary directory ('/tmp/') and a fixed filename ('install.sh') for downloading and executing a script, which can be exploited via symlink attacks.", "vulnerable_example": "work_dir = '/tmp/'\n__salt__['cmd.run'](\n    cmd='curl https://www.serverdensity.com/downloads/agent-install.sh -o install.sh',\n    cwd=work_dir\n)", "fixing_pattern": "The code now uses a secure temporary directory within the application's cache directory and generates a unique filename using `tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile`, preventing symlink attacks.", "fixing_example": "work_dir = os.path.join(__opts__['cachedir'], 'tmp')\nif not os.path.isdir(work_dir):\n    os.mkdir(work_dir)\ninstall_file = tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile(dir=work_dir, suffix='.sh', delete=False)\ninstall_filename = install_file.name\ninstall_file.close()\n__salt__['cmd.run'](\n    cmd='curl https://www.serverdensity.com/downloads/agent-install.sh -o {0}'.format(install_filename),\n    cwd=work_dir\n)"}
{"pattern_name": "Incomplete Error Handling and Resource Cleanup", "functionality": "The code performs a series of initialization and configuration steps, but fails to properly handle errors and clean up resources in case of failures.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code does not ensure proper cleanup of resources (e.g., unbinding ports, freeing options) or logging of errors when a function fails, which can lead to resource leaks or inconsistent states.", "vulnerable_example": "if(bind_ports())\n    return 1;", "fixing_pattern": "The fix ensures that resources are properly cleaned up and errors are logged before returning from a failed function call. This includes unbinding ports, freeing options, and flushing error logs.", "fixing_example": "if(bind_ports()) {\n    unbind_ports();\n    options_free(1);\n    options_defaults();\n    options_apply();\n    log_flush(LOG_MODE_ERROR);\n    return 1;\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure Localhost Check", "functionality": "The code checks if a request originates from localhost by verifying the client's IP address.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using IP address to verify if the request is from localhost without proper validation of the IP source.", "vulnerable_example": "String clientIp = request.getHeader(\"X-Forwarded-For\");\nif (clientIp == null) clientIp = request.getRemoteAddr();\nif (!InetAddress.getByName(clientIp).isLoopbackAddress()) {\n    response.sendError(HttpServletResponse.SC_FORBIDDEN,\n            \"Git hook callbacks can only be accessed from localhost.\");\n    return;\n}", "fixing_pattern": "Replacing the IP-based localhost check with a token-based authentication mechanism to ensure the request is from a trusted source.", "fixing_example": "List<String> fields = StringUtils.splitAndTrim(request.getPathInfo(), \"/\");\nPreconditions.checkState(fields.size() == 3);\nif (!fields.get(2).equals(GitUtils.HOOK_TOKEN)) {\n    response.sendError(HttpServletResponse.SC_FORBIDDEN,\n            \"Git hook callbacks can only be accessed by OneDev itself\");\n    return;\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Hardcoded API Key", "functionality": "The code defines constants for various API keys and URLs used in the application, including a Google Maps API key, to facilitate integration with external services.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The vulnerability arises from hardcoding sensitive information, such as API keys, directly in the source code. This practice exposes the keys to anyone with access to the codebase, making it easier for attackers to misuse them.", "vulnerable_example": "define('LL_STRIPE_KEY', 'sk_test_51JkLZ2SJmQ9WVdrOVREWTJXVnBFTXpBbWNHbzlNQS0tJnM9Y29uc3VtZXJzZWNyZXQmeD02Mw');", "fixing_pattern": "To resolve this issue, sensitive information like API keys should be stored in environment variables or secure configuration files outside the codebase. This ensures that the keys are not exposed in the source code and can be managed securely.", "fixing_example": "define('LL_GMAPS_KEY', getenv('GMAPS_API_KEY'));"}
{"pattern_name": "Incorrect Path Handling in Initialization", "functionality": "The code initializes a repository object by constructing paths using directory and file name components.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using `os.path.dirname` and `os.path.basename` separately to construct paths can lead to incorrect path handling if the input path structure changes.", "vulnerable_example": "RdiffRepo.__init__(self, os.path.dirname(p), os.path.basename(p), encoding='utf-8')", "fixing_pattern": "Directly passing the complete path to the initialization function instead of splitting it into components.", "fixing_example": "RdiffRepo.__init__(self, p, encoding='utf-8')"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure Configuration Retrieval", "functionality": "Retrieves configuration settings for HTTP/2 from the server or directory context.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Directly retrieves configuration without proper validation or fallback mechanisms, potentially leading to null or misconfigured settings.", "vulnerable_example": "const h2_config *h2_config_rget(request_rec *r) {\n    h2_config *cfg = (h2_config *)ap_get_module_config(r->per_dir_config, &http2_module);\n    return cfg? cfg : h2_config_sget(r->server);\n}", "fixing_pattern": "Replaces the insecure configuration retrieval with a function that explicitly handles and validates configuration settings, ensuring proper defaults and bounds.", "fixing_example": "void h2_get_num_workers(server_rec *s, int *minw, int *maxw) {\n    int threads_per_child = 0;\n    *minw = h2_config_sgeti(s, H2_CONF_MIN_WORKERS);\n    *maxw = h2_config_sgeti(s, H2_CONF_MAX_WORKERS);\n    ap_mpm_query(AP_MPMQ_MAX_THREADS, &threads_per_child);\n    if (*minw <= 0) {\n        *minw = threads_per_child;\n    }\n    if (*maxw <= 0) {\n        *maxw = 3 * (*minw) / 2;\n        if (*maxw < 4) {\n            *maxw = 4;\n        }\n    }\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Hardcoded API Key", "functionality": "The code populates default settings into a database, including an API key for Google Maps, a blog title, and pagination settings. It iterates through a predefined list of settings and updates or inserts them into the database.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The vulnerability arises from hardcoding sensitive information, such as an API key, directly into the source code. This practice exposes the key to anyone with access to the codebase, increasing the risk of unauthorized use or abuse.", "vulnerable_example": "public function populateDefaultSettings()\n{\n    $settings = [\n        [\n            'name' => 'googlemap_api',\n            'value' => 'AIzaSyCRqfUKokTWoFg77sAhHOBew_NLgepcTOM',\n        ],\n        [\n            'name' => 'blog_title',\n            'value' => 'Blog Home',\n        ],\n    ];\n\n    foreach ($settings as $setting) {\n        DB::table('settings')->updateOrInsert(\n            ['name' => $setting['name']],\n            ['value' => $setting['value']]\n        );\n    }\n}", "fixing_pattern": "To resolve this vulnerability, sensitive information like API keys should be stored in environment variables or secure configuration files, rather than hardcoded in the source code. This ensures that the key is not exposed in the codebase and can be managed securely.", "fixing_example": "public function populateDefaultSettings()\n{\n    $settings = [\n        [\n            'name' => 'googlemap_api',\n            'value' => env('GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY'),\n        ],\n        [\n            'name' => 'blog_title',\n            'value' => 'Blog Home',\n        ],\n    ];\n\n    foreach ($settings as $setting) {\n        DB::table('settings')->updateOrInsert(\n            ['name' => $setting['name']],\n            ['value' => $setting['value']]\n        );\n    }\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Missing CSRF Protection in HTTP POST Handler", "functionality": "The code handles user registration by accepting a POST request with user registration data, validating the input, checking for existing users, and adding a new user to the repository.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The HTTP POST handler lacks the `[ValidateAntiForgeryToken]` attribute, making it vulnerable to Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) attacks. Without this attribute, the application cannot verify that the request originated from an authenticated user, allowing malicious actors to forge requests and perform unauthorized actions.", "vulnerable_example": "[HttpPost]\npublic IActionResult ChangePassword([FromBody] ChangePasswordModel model)\n{\n    if (model == null)\n    {\n        return BadRequest(\"Invalid password data.\");\n    }\n\n    userRepository.ChangePassword(model);\n    return Ok(\"Password changed successfully.\");\n}", "fixing_pattern": "To mitigate CSRF vulnerabilities, the `[ValidateAntiForgeryToken]` attribute should be added to the HTTP POST handler. This ensures that the request includes a valid anti-forgery token, verifying its authenticity and preventing unauthorized requests.", "fixing_example": "[HttpPost]\n[ValidateAntiForgeryToken]\npublic IActionResult RegisterUser([FromBody] UserRegistrationModel userRegistration)\n{\n    if (userRegistration == null)\n    {\n        return BadRequest(\"Invalid user registration data.\");\n    }\n\n    if (!ModelState.IsValid)\n    {\n        return BadRequest(ModelState);\n    }\n\n    if (userRepository.UserExists(userRegistration.Username))\n    {\n        return BadRequest(\"Username already exists.\");\n    }\n\n    if (!userRepository.IsEmailValid(userRegistration.Email))\n    {\n        return BadRequest(\"Invalid email address.\");\n    }\n\n    var user = new User\n    {\n        Username = userRegistration.Username,\n        Email = userRegistration.Email,\n        PasswordHash = HashPassword(userRegistration.Password)\n    };\n\n    userRepository.AddUser(user);\n    return Ok(\"User registered successfully.\");\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure Session Management", "functionality": "The code handles user login and session management, including setting session data after a successful login.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The session data is stored without any integrity verification, making it susceptible to session hijacking if the session cookie is compromised.", "vulnerable_example": "flask.session['usersession.id'] = u.session", "fixing_pattern": "A session signature is added to the session data to ensure its integrity. This signature is generated using a function that combines the user ID and session ID, making it harder for an attacker to forge or tamper with the session.", "fixing_example": "flask.session['usersession.signature'] = session_signature(u.get_id(), u.session)"}
{"pattern_name": "Unrestricted Application Property Routing", "functionality": "The code uses application properties to determine the routing expression for function execution. These properties can be used to dynamically construct URLs or other resources.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The routing expression from application properties is passed directly to a function without validation or restriction, allowing potential manipulation of the expression to bypass URL restrictions.", "vulnerable_example": "function = this.functionFromExpression(functionProperties.getRoutingExpression(), message);", "fixing_pattern": "The routing expression from application properties is now passed with an additional boolean parameter to enforce validation or restrictions, preventing unauthorized URL access.", "fixing_example": "function = this.functionFromExpression(functionProperties.getRoutingExpression(), message, false);"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure Token Secret Usage", "functionality": "The code generates a JWT token using a secret key for signing.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using a hardcoded or insecure secret key for token generation, which could be compromised.", "vulnerable_example": "Algorithm algorithm = Algorithm.HMAC256(SECRET);", "fixing_pattern": "Using a secure, configurable secret key for token generation instead of a hardcoded value.", "fixing_example": "Algorithm algorithm = Algorithm.HMAC256(tokenSecret);"}
{"pattern_name": "Hardcoded Credentials in Test Code", "functionality": "The code tests the login functionality of a web application by sending HTTP POST requests with both invalid and valid credentials to a login endpoint. It then checks the response for expected error messages or successful login indicators.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code hardcodes sensitive credentials (usernames and passwords) directly in the source code. This practice is insecure because it exposes credentials to anyone with access to the codebase, making it easier for attackers to exploit them.", "vulnerable_example": "def test_login_functionality():\n    username = 'user'\n    password = 'password'\n    response = requests.post('https://example.com/login', data={'username': username, 'password': password})", "fixing_pattern": "To resolve this issue, credentials should be stored securely using a secrets management system or environment variables. This ensures that sensitive information is not embedded in the code and can be managed securely.", "fixing_example": "import os\n\ndef test_login_functionality():\n    username = os.getenv('TEST_USERNAME')\n    password = os.getenv('TEST_PASSWORD')\n    response = requests.post('https://example.com/login', data={'username': username, 'password': password})"}
{"pattern_name": "Unsafe YAML Implicit Resolver", "functionality": "The code uses YAML implicit resolvers to handle environment variables in the configuration files.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using `yaml.UnsafeLoader` for implicit resolution, which can lead to arbitrary code execution during YAML parsing.", "vulnerable_example": "yaml.add_implicit_resolver('!env_var', IMPLICIT_ENV_VAR_MATCHER, Loader=yaml.UnsafeLoader)", "fixing_pattern": "Replacing `yaml.UnsafeLoader` with `yaml.SafeLoader` for implicit resolution to prevent arbitrary code execution.", "fixing_example": "yaml.add_implicit_resolver('!env_var', IMPLICIT_ENV_VAR_MATCHER, Loader=yaml.SafeLoader)"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure Configuration Initialization", "functionality": "The code initializes a configuration object for handling system settings and authentication.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Directly initializing configuration using a module-level function without proper access control checks.", "vulnerable_example": "self.rhsmcfg = rhsm.config.initConfig()", "fixing_pattern": "Wrapping the configuration initialization in a controlled class instance to enforce access control.", "fixing_example": "self.rhsmcfg = config.Config(initConfig())"}
{"pattern_name": "Incorrect Role-Permission Mapping", "functionality": "The code defines permissions for plugin management operations and maps them to specific roles.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Mapping a sensitive permission ('LIST') to a role ('manage') that might have broader access than intended.", "vulnerable_example": "{\n    \"key\": \"LIST\",\n    \"name\": \"List plugins\",\n    \"description\": gettext(\"Allows to list installed plugins.\"),\n    \"default_groups\": [READONLY_GROUP, USER_GROUP, ADMIN_GROUP],\n    \"roles\": [\"manage\"],\n}", "fixing_pattern": "Changing the role mapping to a more specific role ('list') that better matches the permission's scope.", "fixing_example": "{\n    \"key\": \"LIST\",\n    \"name\": \"List plugins\",\n    \"description\": gettext(\"Allows to list installed plugins.\"),\n    \"default_groups\": [READONLY_GROUP, USER_GROUP, ADMIN_GROUP],\n    \"roles\": [\"list\"],\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure Script Execution", "functionality": "The code executes a script downloaded from a remote server.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code executes a script with a fixed filename ('install.sh') in a hardcoded temporary directory, which can be exploited via symlink attacks.", "vulnerable_example": "__salt__['cmd.run'](\n    cmd='./install.sh -a {account_url} -k {agent_key}'.format(\n        account_url=account_url, agent_key=agent_key),\n    cwd=work_dir\n)", "fixing_pattern": "The code now executes the script using the unique filename generated by `tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile`, ensuring that the script executed is the one that was downloaded.", "fixing_example": "__salt__['cmd.run'](\n    cmd='./{filename} -a {account_url} -k {agent_key}'.format(\n        filename=install_filename, account_url=account_url, agent_key=agent_key),\n    cwd=work_dir\n)"}
{"pattern_name": "Command Injection via Untrusted File Execution", "functionality": "The code downloads a file from a user-provided URL, saves it to a temporary location, and then attempts to execute the file using the system's default application.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The vulnerability arises from directly executing a file whose content and origin are controlled by an untrusted source (the user-provided URL). This can lead to command injection if the file contains malicious code or if the file's execution triggers unintended system commands.", "vulnerable_example": "string tempFilePath = Path.GetTempFileName();\nProcess.Start(tempFilePath);", "fixing_pattern": "To mitigate this vulnerability, avoid executing files from untrusted sources. Instead, validate the file's content, restrict the file types that can be executed, or use safer methods to handle the file (e.g., opening it in a read-only mode or displaying its content without execution).", "fixing_example": "string tempFilePath = Path.GetTempFileName();\nif (IsFileTypeSafe(tempFilePath)) // Custom validation function\n{\n    Process.Start(tempFilePath);\n}\nelse\n{\n    Console.WriteLine(\"Unsafe file type detected. Execution aborted.\");\n}"}
