{"pattern_name": "Hardcoded Template Paths", "functionality": "The code defines Django views for handling password reset functionality, including confirmation and completion. It uses hardcoded template paths to render the appropriate HTML pages for these views.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code directly hardcodes the paths to the template files within the class definitions. This practice is insecure because it embeds configuration details directly in the code, making it difficult to change without modifying the source and potentially exposing sensitive paths.", "vulnerable_example": "class ResetPasswordConfirmView(BaseAdminView):\n    password_reset_confirm_template = 'xadmin/auth/password_reset/confirm.html'\n\nclass ResetPasswordCompleteView(BaseAdminView):\n    password_reset_complete_template = 'xadmin/auth/password_reset/complete.html'", "fixing_pattern": "To resolve this issue, the template paths should be moved to a configuration file or environment variables. This allows for easier management and updates without modifying the source code, enhancing security and flexibility.", "fixing_example": "from django.conf import settings\n\nclass ResetPasswordConfirmView(BaseAdminView):\n    password_reset_confirm_template = settings.PASSWORD_RESET_CONFIRM_TEMPLATE\n\nclass ResetPasswordCompleteView(BaseAdminView):\n    password_reset_complete_template = settings.PASSWORD_RESET_COMPLETE_TEMPLATE"}
{"pattern_name": "Incorrect Path Handling in Initialization", "functionality": "The code initializes a repository object by constructing paths using directory and file name components.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using `os.path.dirname` and `os.path.basename` separately to construct paths can lead to incorrect path handling if the input path structure changes.", "vulnerable_example": "RdiffRepo.__init__(self, os.path.dirname(p), os.path.basename(p), encoding='utf-8')", "fixing_pattern": "Directly passing the complete path to the initialization function instead of splitting it into components.", "fixing_example": "RdiffRepo.__init__(self, p, encoding='utf-8')"}
{"pattern_name": "Unsafe Context Object Usage", "functionality": "The code uses a context object (h2_ctx) to retrieve server configuration settings, which can lead to misconfiguration if the context is not properly initialized or synchronized.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using a context object (h2_ctx) to retrieve server configuration settings, which can lead to misconfiguration if the context is not properly initialized or synchronized.", "vulnerable_example": "session->s = h2_ctx_server_get(ctx);\nsession->config = h2_config_sget(session->s);\nsession->max_stream_count = h2_config_geti(session->config, H2_CONF_MAX_STREAMS);", "fixing_pattern": "Directly passing the server configuration settings as a parameter to the function, ensuring that the configuration is consistent and properly initialized.", "fixing_example": "session->s = s;\nsession->max_stream_count = h2_config_sgeti(s, H2_CONF_MAX_STREAMS);"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure Configuration Handling", "functionality": "The code handles configuration settings for server and proxy connections, including insecure settings.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Directly setting insecure configuration values without proper validation or access control.", "vulnerable_example": "cfg.set(\"server\", \"insecure\", \"1\")", "fixing_pattern": "Using a more secure configuration handling mechanism that ensures proper validation and access control.", "fixing_example": "conf[\"server\"][\"insecure\"] = \"1\""}
{"pattern_name": "Inconsistent Attribute Default Handling", "functionality": "The code handles default values and related attributes for model fields, including compute, related, and company-dependent attributes.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code inconsistently handles the 'compute_sudo' attribute, which is not always set based on related attributes, potentially leading to inconsistent security contexts.", "vulnerable_example": "if 'related_sudo' in attrs:\n    attrs['compute_sudo'] = attrs['related_sudo']", "fixing_pattern": "The fix ensures that 'compute_sudo' is consistently set based on the 'store' or 'related_sudo' attributes, maintaining a consistent security context.", "fixing_example": "attrs['store'] = store = attrs.get('store', False)\nattrs['compute_sudo'] = attrs.get('compute_sudo', store)"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure Password Hashing Without Salt", "functionality": "The code creates a new user by validating required fields, hashing the password, synchronizing timestamps, retrieving a Gravatar image, and inserting the user data into a database.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code uses `password_hash` with `PASSWORD_DEFAULT`, which does not explicitly enforce the use of a salt. While `password_hash` internally generates a salt, the lack of explicit salt management can lead to misunderstandings or insecure practices in other implementations. Additionally, the code does not verify the strength of the hashing algorithm or ensure it is up-to-date.", "vulnerable_example": "function createUser(array $userData) {\n    if (!isset($userData['password'])) {\n        throw new InvalidArgumentException('Password is required.');\n    }\n    $hashedPassword = md5($userData['password']); // Insecure hashing without salt\n    // ...\n}", "fixing_pattern": "To fix the vulnerability, ensure the password is hashed using a secure algorithm like bcrypt, which inherently includes a salt. Additionally, verify that the hashing algorithm is up-to-date and meets current security standards.", "fixing_example": "function createUser(array $userData) {\n    if (!isset($userData['password'])) {\n        throw new InvalidArgumentException('Password is required.');\n    }\n    $hashedPassword = password_hash($userData['password'], PASSWORD_BCRYPT); // Secure hashing with bcrypt\n    // ...\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Excessive User Information Exposure", "functionality": "The code loads and exposes detailed user information, including group memberships and leadership roles, which is then made available in the web client context.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Exposing sensitive user information (e.g., group memberships, leadership roles) in the web client context without proper justification or need.", "vulnerable_example": "The code loads and exposes detailed user information such as `leader_of_groups` and other user-related data in the context dictionary, which is then used in the web client.", "fixing_pattern": "Removing unnecessary user information from the context to minimize exposure of sensitive data.", "fixing_example": "The commit removes unnecessary user information from the context, ensuring that only essential data is exposed. For example, the addition of `member_of_groups` is carefully considered and added only if necessary."}
{"pattern_name": "Insufficient Session Expiration", "functionality": "The code handles user login and session management, including creating new users and updating user attributes.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code uses `add()` method to save user objects, which may not properly handle session expiration or persistence, leading to potential session management issues.", "vulnerable_example": "userobj.add()", "fixing_pattern": "The code is updated to use `commit()` method instead of `add()` to ensure proper session management and persistence, which helps in enforcing session expiration policies.", "fixing_example": "userobj.commit()"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure URL Path Construction", "functionality": "The code constructs a URL path by combining a base path prefix with a user-provided path, potentially allowing path manipulation.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code blindly accepts and processes user-provided path without proper validation or sanitization, potentially allowing path traversal or manipulation.", "vulnerable_example": "def _build_url(self, path, queries=()):\n    if not path.startswith(self.path_prefix):\n        if path.startswith('/'):\n            path = path[1:]\n        path = '/'.join((self.path_prefix, path))", "fixing_pattern": "Added a parameter to explicitly control whether the path prefix should be ignored, allowing for more secure handling of path construction.", "fixing_example": "def _build_url(self, path, queries, ignore_prefix):\n    if not path.startswith(self.path_prefix) and not ignore_prefix:\n        if path.startswith('/'):\n            path = path[1:]\n        path = '/'.join((self.path_prefix, path))"}
{"pattern_name": "Hardcoded Endpoint Path", "functionality": "The code defines a hardcoded endpoint path for the Cloud Foundry application, which is used to map and handle requests.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using a hardcoded string for the endpoint path, which can lead to security bypass if the path is predictable or known.", "vulnerable_example": "return new CloudFoundryWebFluxEndpointHandlerMapping(new EndpointMapping(\"/cloudfoundryapplication\"), webEndpoints, endpointMediaTypes, getCorsConfiguration(), securityInterceptor, new EndpointLinksResolver(allEndpoints));", "fixing_pattern": "Replacing the hardcoded endpoint path with a constant or configurable value to reduce predictability and improve security.", "fixing_example": "return new CloudFoundryWebFluxEndpointHandlerMapping(new EndpointMapping(BASE_PATH), webEndpoints, endpointMediaTypes, getCorsConfiguration(), securityInterceptor, allEndpoints);"}
{"pattern_name": "Unsafe Template Environment Usage", "functionality": "The code uses a template environment to render an HTML template with dynamic data.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using a default or unsafe template environment that may allow injection of malicious content.", "vulnerable_example": "template = env.get_template('generate_new.html')", "fixing_pattern": "Replacing the default template environment with a specifically designated unsafe environment to mitigate injection risks.", "fixing_example": "template = unsafe_env.get_template('generate_new.html')"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure Temporary File Path Handling", "functionality": "The code processes file uploads and handles temporary file paths, including sanitizing file names to ensure they are safe for use in the filesystem.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code does not properly handle spaces in temporary file paths, which can lead to misinterpretation of the path and unintended file operations (e.g., deletion).", "vulnerable_example": "file_name = force_text(file_name, encoding, errors='replace')\nfile_name = self.sanitize_file_name(file_name)", "fixing_pattern": "The fix ensures that spaces in temporary file paths are properly handled by adding additional checks and safeguards to prevent misinterpretation of the path.", "fixing_example": "file_name = force_text(file_name, encoding, errors='replace')\nfile_name = self.sanitize_file_name(file_name)\n# Additional checks and safeguards are added to handle spaces in paths"}
{"pattern_name": "Unrestricted File Upload Without Validation", "functionality": "The code processes file uploads by retrieving file paths from a request and directly fetching the files from storage without validating the file type or origin.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code accepts file paths from user input and directly fetches the files from storage without any validation or verification of the file's safety or origin.", "vulnerable_example": "request.FILES.setlist(field_name, list(self.get_files_from_storage(paths)))", "fixing_pattern": "The fix introduces validation by requiring a signature for the file upload and checking for suspicious file operations. This ensures that only authorized and safe files are processed.", "fixing_example": "request.FILES.setlist(field_name, list(self.get_files_from_storage(paths, signature)))"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure Parameter Handling in Test Cases", "functionality": "The test cases handle parameters that could be manipulated to execute arbitrary code.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The test cases include parameters that are not properly sanitized, making them susceptible to injection attacks.", "vulnerable_example": "assertPlotParam(\"smooth\", \"unwrap\");", "fixing_pattern": "The fix involves updating the test cases to handle parameters securely by adding validation and sanitization checks.", "fixing_example": "assertInvalidPlotParam(\"fgcolor\", \"%5BDEADBE\");"}
{"pattern_name": "Overly Complex Setup with Potential Security Risks", "functionality": "The code sets up a complex environment for testing, including mock objects and configurations for various components like identity, certificates, and DBus interfaces.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The setup method initializes multiple components and mocks, including DBus interfaces, without clear access control mechanisms. This complexity can lead to unintended exposure of sensitive methods or data.", "vulnerable_example": "def setUp(self):\n    self.addCleanup(patch.stopall)\n    cfg_patcher = patch.object(subscription_manager.managercli, 'cfg', new=stubs.config.CFG)\n    self.mock_cfg = cfg_patcher.start()\n    id_mock = NonCallableMock(name='FixtureIdentityMock')\n    id_mock.exists_and_valid = Mock(return_value=True)\n    id_mock.uuid = 'fixture_identity_mock_uuid'\n    id_mock.name = 'fixture_identity_mock_name'\n    id_mock.cert_dir_path = \"/not/a/real/path/to/pki/consumer/\"\n    id_mock.keypath.return_value = \"/not/a/real/key/path\"\n    id_mock.certpath.return_value = \"/not/a/real/cert/path\"\n    self.mock_calc = NonCallableMock()\n    self.mock_calc.calculate.return_value = None\n    self.mock_repofile_path_exists_patcher = patch('subscription_manager.repolib.RepoFile.path_exists')\n    mock_repofile_path_exists = self.mock_repofile_path_exists_patcher.start()\n    mock_repofile_path_exists.return_value = True\n    inj.provide(inj.IDENTITY, id_mock)\n    inj.provide(inj.PRODUCT_DATE_RANGE_CALCULATOR, self.mock_calc)\n    inj.provide(inj.ENTITLEMENT_STATUS_CACHE, stubs.StubEntitlementStatusCache())\n    inj.provide(inj.PROD_STATUS_CACHE, stubs.StubProductStatusCache())\n    inj.provide(inj.OVERRIDE_STATUS_CACHE, stubs.StubOverrideStatusCache())\n    inj.provide(inj.RELEASE_STATUS_CACHE, stubs.StubReleaseStatusCache())\n    inj.provide(inj.PROFILE_MANAGER, stubs.StubProfileManager())\n    self.ent_dir = stubs.StubEntitlementDirectory()\n    inj.provide(inj.ENT_DIR, self.ent_dir)\n    self.prod_dir = stubs.StubProductDirectory()\n    inj.provide(inj.PROD_DIR, self.prod_dir)\n    inj.provide(inj.INSTALLED_PRODUCTS_MANAGER, stubs.StubInstalledProductsManager())\n    self.stub_cp_provider = stubs.StubCPProvider()\n    self._release_versions = []\n    self.stub_cp_provider.content_connection.get_versions = self._get_release_versions\n    inj.provide(inj.CP_PROVIDER, self.stub_cp_provider)\n    inj.provide(inj.CERT_SORTER, stubs.StubCertSorter())\n    plugin_manager_mock = MagicMock(name='FixturePluginManagerMock')\n    plugin_manager_mock.runiter.return_value = iter([])\n    inj.provide(inj.PLUGIN_MANAGER, plugin_manager_mock)\n    inj.provide(inj.DBUS_IFACE, Mock(name='FixtureDbusIfaceMock'))\n    pooltype_cache = Mock()\n    inj.provide(inj.POOLTYPE_CACHE, pooltype_cache)\n    inj.provide(inj.ACTION_LOCK, RLock)\n    self.stub_facts = stubs.StubFacts()\n    inj.provide(inj.FACTS, self.stub_facts)\n    self.dbus_patcher = patch('subscription_manager.managercli.CliCommand._request_validity_check')\n    self.dbus_patcher.start()\n    self.is_valid_server_patcher = patch(\"subscription_manager.managercli.is_valid_server_info\")\n    is_valid_server_mock = self.is_valid_server_patcher.start()\n    is_valid_server_mock.return_value = True\n    self.test_proxy_connection_patcher = patch(\"subscription_manager.managercli.CliCommand.test_proxy_connection\")\n    test_proxy_connection_mock = self.test_proxy_connection_patcher.start()\n    test_proxy_connection_mock.return_value = True\n    self.files_to_cleanup = []", "fixing_pattern": "The fix simplifies the setup by removing the complex initialization of multiple components and mocks, reducing the attack surface and potential security risks.", "fixing_example": "def set_facts(self):\n    return {\"mock.facts\": \"true\"}"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure Cloud Foundry Environment Variable Handling", "functionality": "The application uses environment variables provided by Cloud Foundry to determine security configurations or access controls.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The application trusts environment variables without proper validation or sanitization, potentially allowing an attacker to manipulate these variables to bypass security checks.", "vulnerable_example": "String cloudFoundryEnv = System.getenv(\"CLOUD_FOUNDRY_ENV\");\nif (cloudFoundryEnv != null && cloudFoundryEnv.equals(\"production\")) {\n    // Bypass security checks\n}", "fixing_pattern": "The fix involves validating and sanitizing environment variables before using them in security-critical decisions. Additionally, the application should enforce strict checks and not rely solely on environment variables for security configurations.", "fixing_example": "String cloudFoundryEnv = System.getenv(\"CLOUD_FOUNDRY_ENV\");\nif (cloudFoundryEnv != null && cloudFoundryEnv.equals(\"production\")) {\n    // Perform additional security checks\n    if (isValidEnvironment(cloudFoundryEnv)) {\n        // Proceed with secure operations\n    }\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure Configuration Update via DBus Interface", "functionality": "The code updates server configuration settings (hostname, port, prefix) using a global configuration object (CFG) that is accessible via a DBus interface.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Directly updating sensitive configuration settings using a global configuration object without proper access control checks.", "vulnerable_example": "CFG.set('server', 'hostname', hostname)\nCFG.set('server', 'port', port)\nCFG.set('server', 'prefix', prefix)", "fixing_pattern": "Updating configuration settings using a local configuration dictionary (conf) instead of a global configuration object, thereby limiting access to sensitive settings.", "fixing_example": "conf['server']['hostname'] = hostname\nconf['server']['port'] = port\nconf['server']['prefix'] = prefix"}
{"pattern_name": "Incomplete Metadata Validation", "functionality": "The code is responsible for validating that the permissions displayed to the user during app installation match the actual permissions granted at runtime by comparing metadata from different sources.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code fails to properly handle metadata containing null bytes, leading to incomplete validation of permissions. The validation process stops at the first null byte, allowing hidden permissions to be granted.", "vulnerable_example": "static void\nresolve_op_end (FlatpakTransaction *self,\n                FlatpakTransactionOperation *op,\n                const char *checksum,\n                GFile *sideload_path,\n                GBytes *metadata_bytes)", "fixing_pattern": "The fix introduces proper error handling and ensures that the metadata validation process is complete by checking the return value of the validation function and propagating any errors.", "fixing_example": "static gboolean\nresolve_op_end (FlatpakTransaction *self,\n                FlatpakTransactionOperation *op,\n                const char *checksum,\n                GFile *sideload_path,\n                GBytes *metadata_bytes,\n                GError **error)"}
{"pattern_name": "Unnecessary Resource Initialization on Connection", "functionality": "The code initializes backend plugins and prepares them for each new connection, even if the connection might not proceed to the actual request processing phase.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code performs backend initialization (`backend_open` and `backend_prepare`) immediately after establishing a connection, regardless of whether the connection will be used for actual requests.", "vulnerable_example": "lock_request (conn);\nr = backend_open (backend, conn, read_only);\nunlock_request (conn);\nif (r == -1)\n  goto done;\nlock_request (conn);\nif (backend)\n  r = backend_prepare (backend, conn);\nelse\n  r = 0;\nunlock_request (conn);\nif (r == -1)\n  goto done;", "fixing_pattern": "The fix removes the unnecessary backend initialization (`backend_open` and `backend_prepare`) steps during the connection phase, deferring them until they are actually needed for processing requests.", "fixing_example": ""}
{"pattern_name": "Conditional Resource Initialization", "functionality": "The code initializes a resource (dump directory) based on a configuration setting.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The resource is initialized unconditionally, which could lead to unintended behavior if the configuration setting is not properly checked.", "vulnerable_example": "struct dump_dir *dd = create_dump_dir_from_problem_data(pd, g_settings_dump_location);", "fixing_pattern": "The resource initialization is made conditional based on a configuration setting, ensuring that the resource is only created when appropriate.", "fixing_example": "struct dump_dir *dd = NULL;\nif (g_settings_privatereports)\n    dd = create_dump_dir_from_problem_data_ext(pd, g_settings_dump_location, 0);\nelse\n    dd = create_dump_dir_from_problem_data(pd, g_settings_dump_location);"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure Configuration Retrieval", "functionality": "Retrieves configuration settings for HTTP/2 from the server or directory context.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Directly retrieves configuration without proper validation or fallback mechanisms, potentially leading to null or misconfigured settings.", "vulnerable_example": "const h2_config *h2_config_rget(request_rec *r) {\n    h2_config *cfg = (h2_config *)ap_get_module_config(r->per_dir_config, &http2_module);\n    return cfg? cfg : h2_config_sget(r->server);\n}", "fixing_pattern": "Replaces the insecure configuration retrieval with a function that explicitly handles and validates configuration settings, ensuring proper defaults and bounds.", "fixing_example": "void h2_get_num_workers(server_rec *s, int *minw, int *maxw) {\n    int threads_per_child = 0;\n    *minw = h2_config_sgeti(s, H2_CONF_MIN_WORKERS);\n    *maxw = h2_config_sgeti(s, H2_CONF_MAX_WORKERS);\n    ap_mpm_query(AP_MPMQ_MAX_THREADS, &threads_per_child);\n    if (*minw <= 0) {\n        *minw = threads_per_child;\n    }\n    if (*maxw <= 0) {\n        *maxw = 3 * (*minw) / 2;\n        if (*maxw < 4) {\n            *maxw = 4;\n        }\n    }\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Hardcoded API Key", "functionality": "The code populates default settings into a database, including an API key for Google Maps, a blog title, and pagination settings. It iterates through a predefined list of settings and updates or inserts them into the database.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The vulnerability arises from hardcoding sensitive information, such as an API key, directly into the source code. This practice exposes the key to anyone with access to the codebase, increasing the risk of unauthorized use or abuse.", "vulnerable_example": "public function populateDefaultSettings()\n{\n    $settings = [\n        [\n            'name' => 'googlemap_api',\n            'value' => 'AIzaSyCRqfUKokTWoFg77sAhHOBew_NLgepcTOM',\n        ],\n        [\n            'name' => 'blog_title',\n            'value' => 'Blog Home',\n        ],\n    ];\n\n    foreach ($settings as $setting) {\n        DB::table('settings')->updateOrInsert(\n            ['name' => $setting['name']],\n            ['value' => $setting['value']]\n        );\n    }\n}", "fixing_pattern": "To resolve this vulnerability, sensitive information like API keys should be stored in environment variables or secure configuration files, rather than hardcoded in the source code. This ensures that the key is not exposed in the codebase and can be managed securely.", "fixing_example": "public function populateDefaultSettings()\n{\n    $settings = [\n        [\n            'name' => 'googlemap_api',\n            'value' => env('GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY'),\n        ],\n        [\n            'name' => 'blog_title',\n            'value' => 'Blog Home',\n        ],\n    ];\n\n    foreach ($settings as $setting) {\n        DB::table('settings')->updateOrInsert(\n            ['name' => $setting['name']],\n            ['value' => $setting['value']]\n        );\n    }\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Missing Resource Limitation", "functionality": "The code processes input data (e.g., JSON, YAML) without enforcing limits on the size or complexity of the data structure.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The function processes input data without restricting the number of nodes or depth of the data structure, which could lead to resource exhaustion or unexpected behavior.", "vulnerable_example": "def remarshal(input, output, input_format, output_format, json_indent, ordered, stringify, unwrap, wrap, yaml_options):\n    # Process input data without limiting the number of nodes", "fixing_pattern": "Add a parameter to enforce a maximum limit on the number of nodes or depth of the data structure to prevent resource exhaustion or abuse.", "fixing_example": "def remarshal(input, output, input_format, output_format, json_indent, max_nodes, ordered, stringify, unwrap, wrap, yaml_options):\n    # Process input data with a limit on the number of nodes"}
{"pattern_name": "Unescaped User-Generated Content in HTML Output", "functionality": "The code retrieves user or group properties (e.g., fullname or title) and directly includes them in the output without proper escaping, which is then rendered in a web page.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Directly embedding user-generated content into HTML output without escaping it, making it susceptible to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks.", "vulnerable_example": "principals.append(user.getProperty('fullname', None) or user.getId())", "fixing_pattern": "Applying HTML escaping to user-generated content before including it in the output to prevent XSS vulnerabilities.", "fixing_example": "principals.append(escape(user.getProperty('fullname', None)) or user.getId())"}
{"pattern_name": "Unrestricted Application Property Routing", "functionality": "The code uses application properties to determine the routing expression for function execution. These properties can be used to dynamically construct URLs or other resources.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The routing expression from application properties is passed directly to a function without validation or restriction, allowing potential manipulation of the expression to bypass URL restrictions.", "vulnerable_example": "function = this.functionFromExpression(functionProperties.getRoutingExpression(), message);", "fixing_pattern": "The routing expression from application properties is now passed with an additional boolean parameter to enforce validation or restrictions, preventing unauthorized URL access.", "fixing_example": "function = this.functionFromExpression(functionProperties.getRoutingExpression(), message, false);"}
{"pattern_name": "Matrix Variable Path Handling", "functionality": "The code constructs a URI from an HTTP request's scheme, authority, and path. It is used to route requests to the appropriate service or controller.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code directly uses the request path without considering matrix variables, which can lead to incorrect routing or bypassing of security checks.", "vulnerable_example": "return URI.create(scheme + \"://\" + authority + req.path());", "fixing_pattern": "The fix involves retrieving the path with matrix variables from the request context and appending the query string if it exists, ensuring that the URI is correctly constructed and includes all necessary components.", "fixing_example": "String path = requestTarget.maybePathWithMatrixVariables();\nif (requestTarget.query() != null) {\n    path = path + '?' + requestTarget.query();\n}\nreturn URI.create(scheme + \"://\" + authority + path);"}
{"pattern_name": "Unescaped HTML Output in Template Rendering", "functionality": "The code dynamically generates HTML content by inserting user-controlled data into a string template, which is then rendered in the browser.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Directly embedding user-controlled data into HTML without proper escaping or sanitization, allowing potential injection of malicious scripts.", "vulnerable_example": "String.format(\"<li class=\\\"nav-item\\\" data-id=\\\"%s\\\"><a class=\\\"nav-link\\\" href=\\\"%s\\\">%s</a></li>\", useNav, url, name)", "fixing_pattern": "Applying proper escaping or sanitization to user-controlled data before embedding it in HTML templates. In this case, the fix adds a CSS class 'text-ellipsis' which helps mitigate XSS by limiting the display of potentially malicious content.", "fixing_example": "String.format(\"<li class=\\\"nav-item\\\" data-id=\\\"%s\\\"><a class=\\\"nav-link text-ellipsis\\\" href=\\\"%s\\\">%s</a></li>\", useNav, url, name)"}
{"pattern_name": "Unrestricted Image Format Processing", "functionality": "The code processes an uploaded image file without restricting the allowed image formats.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using Image.open() without specifying allowed formats, which could potentially allow processing of unsafe or unexpected image formats.", "vulnerable_example": "image = Image.open(file)", "fixing_pattern": "Restricting image processing to specific, safe formats by using the formats parameter in Image.open().", "fixing_example": "image = Image.open(file, formats=settings.PILLOW_FORMATS_QUESTIONS_IMAGE)"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure Configuration Initialization", "functionality": "The code initializes a configuration object for handling system settings and authentication.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Directly initializing configuration using a module-level function without proper access control checks.", "vulnerable_example": "self.rhsmcfg = rhsm.config.initConfig()", "fixing_pattern": "Wrapping the configuration initialization in a controlled class instance to enforce access control.", "fixing_example": "self.rhsmcfg = config.Config(initConfig())"}
