{"pattern_name": "Unvalidated Server Information", "functionality": "The code processes server information such as hostname, port, and prefix for establishing connections.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Lack of validation for server information before using it to establish connections.", "vulnerable_example": "cfg.set(\"server\", \"hostname\", self.server_hostname)", "fixing_pattern": "Adding validation checks for server information to ensure it is safe and correct before use.", "fixing_example": "conf[\"server\"][\"hostname\"] = self.server_hostname"}
{"pattern_name": "Overly Complex Setup with Potential Security Risks", "functionality": "The code sets up a complex environment for testing, including mock objects and configurations for various components like identity, certificates, and DBus interfaces.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The setup method initializes multiple components and mocks, including DBus interfaces, without clear access control mechanisms. This complexity can lead to unintended exposure of sensitive methods or data.", "vulnerable_example": "def setUp(self):\n    self.addCleanup(patch.stopall)\n    cfg_patcher = patch.object(subscription_manager.managercli, 'cfg', new=stubs.config.CFG)\n    self.mock_cfg = cfg_patcher.start()\n    id_mock = NonCallableMock(name='FixtureIdentityMock')\n    id_mock.exists_and_valid = Mock(return_value=True)\n    id_mock.uuid = 'fixture_identity_mock_uuid'\n    id_mock.name = 'fixture_identity_mock_name'\n    id_mock.cert_dir_path = \"/not/a/real/path/to/pki/consumer/\"\n    id_mock.keypath.return_value = \"/not/a/real/key/path\"\n    id_mock.certpath.return_value = \"/not/a/real/cert/path\"\n    self.mock_calc = NonCallableMock()\n    self.mock_calc.calculate.return_value = None\n    self.mock_repofile_path_exists_patcher = patch('subscription_manager.repolib.RepoFile.path_exists')\n    mock_repofile_path_exists = self.mock_repofile_path_exists_patcher.start()\n    mock_repofile_path_exists.return_value = True\n    inj.provide(inj.IDENTITY, id_mock)\n    inj.provide(inj.PRODUCT_DATE_RANGE_CALCULATOR, self.mock_calc)\n    inj.provide(inj.ENTITLEMENT_STATUS_CACHE, stubs.StubEntitlementStatusCache())\n    inj.provide(inj.PROD_STATUS_CACHE, stubs.StubProductStatusCache())\n    inj.provide(inj.OVERRIDE_STATUS_CACHE, stubs.StubOverrideStatusCache())\n    inj.provide(inj.RELEASE_STATUS_CACHE, stubs.StubReleaseStatusCache())\n    inj.provide(inj.PROFILE_MANAGER, stubs.StubProfileManager())\n    self.ent_dir = stubs.StubEntitlementDirectory()\n    inj.provide(inj.ENT_DIR, self.ent_dir)\n    self.prod_dir = stubs.StubProductDirectory()\n    inj.provide(inj.PROD_DIR, self.prod_dir)\n    inj.provide(inj.INSTALLED_PRODUCTS_MANAGER, stubs.StubInstalledProductsManager())\n    self.stub_cp_provider = stubs.StubCPProvider()\n    self._release_versions = []\n    self.stub_cp_provider.content_connection.get_versions = self._get_release_versions\n    inj.provide(inj.CP_PROVIDER, self.stub_cp_provider)\n    inj.provide(inj.CERT_SORTER, stubs.StubCertSorter())\n    plugin_manager_mock = MagicMock(name='FixturePluginManagerMock')\n    plugin_manager_mock.runiter.return_value = iter([])\n    inj.provide(inj.PLUGIN_MANAGER, plugin_manager_mock)\n    inj.provide(inj.DBUS_IFACE, Mock(name='FixtureDbusIfaceMock'))\n    pooltype_cache = Mock()\n    inj.provide(inj.POOLTYPE_CACHE, pooltype_cache)\n    inj.provide(inj.ACTION_LOCK, RLock)\n    self.stub_facts = stubs.StubFacts()\n    inj.provide(inj.FACTS, self.stub_facts)\n    self.dbus_patcher = patch('subscription_manager.managercli.CliCommand._request_validity_check')\n    self.dbus_patcher.start()\n    self.is_valid_server_patcher = patch(\"subscription_manager.managercli.is_valid_server_info\")\n    is_valid_server_mock = self.is_valid_server_patcher.start()\n    is_valid_server_mock.return_value = True\n    self.test_proxy_connection_patcher = patch(\"subscription_manager.managercli.CliCommand.test_proxy_connection\")\n    test_proxy_connection_mock = self.test_proxy_connection_patcher.start()\n    test_proxy_connection_mock.return_value = True\n    self.files_to_cleanup = []", "fixing_pattern": "The fix simplifies the setup by removing the complex initialization of multiple components and mocks, reducing the attack surface and potential security risks.", "fixing_example": "def set_facts(self):\n    return {\"mock.facts\": \"true\"}"}
{"pattern_name": "Hardcoded Sensitive Identifier", "functionality": "The code uses a hardcoded identifier (project_id) in the API path, which can be manipulated to bypass access restrictions.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using a hardcoded, predictable, or easily guessable identifier in the API path, allowing unauthorized access to resources.", "vulnerable_example": "self.api_path = '/v1.1/123/os-volume-types/1/extra_specs'", "fixing_pattern": "Replacing the hardcoded identifier with a non-sensitive, fake, or dynamically generated value to prevent unauthorized access.", "fixing_example": "self.api_path = '/v1.1/fake/os-volume-types/1/extra_specs'"}
{"pattern_name": "Insufficient Authorization Check in REST API", "functionality": "The code allows users to perform administrative actions (e.g., creating new user accounts) via the REST API without proper authorization checks.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The API endpoint does not verify if the requesting user has the necessary permissions to perform the action.", "vulnerable_example": "public User createUser(User user) {\n    // No authorization check\n    return userRepository.save(user);\n}", "fixing_pattern": "Add proper authorization checks to ensure only users with the required permissions can perform the action.", "fixing_example": "public User createUser(User user) {\n    if (!hasAdminRole()) {\n        throw new AccessDeniedException(\"Insufficient permissions\");\n    }\n    return userRepository.save(user);\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure File Creation", "functionality": "The code creates a file (`app.js`) in a specified directory if it does not already exist, ensuring the directory structure is created with specific permissions.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code creates a file in a directory that could be manipulated by an attacker due to the use of user-supplied input in path construction. The `fs.mkdirSync` and `fs.writeFileSync` functions are used without proper validation, which could lead to the creation of files in unintended locations.", "vulnerable_example": "const userInput = 'malicious/../../etc/passwd';\nconst filePath = path.join('/safe/directory', userInput);\nfs.mkdirSync(path.dirname(filePath), { recursive: true, mode: 0o755 });\nfs.writeFileSync(filePath, 'malicious content');", "fixing_pattern": "To prevent insecure file creation, the code should validate the resolved path to ensure it falls within a safe directory. Additionally, the use of `fs.mkdirSync` and `fs.writeFileSync` should be restricted to paths that have been properly sanitized and validated.", "fixing_example": "const userInput = 'safeInput';\nconst safeBasePath = '/safe/directory';\nconst resolvedPath = path.resolve(safeBasePath, userInput);\nif (resolvedPath.startsWith(safeBasePath)) {\n    fs.mkdirSync(path.dirname(resolvedPath), { recursive: true, mode: 0o755 });\n    fs.writeFileSync(resolvedPath, 'safe content');\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Unsafe Context Object Usage", "functionality": "The code uses a context object (h2_ctx) to retrieve server configuration settings, which can lead to misconfiguration if the context is not properly initialized or synchronized.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using a context object (h2_ctx) to retrieve server configuration settings, which can lead to misconfiguration if the context is not properly initialized or synchronized.", "vulnerable_example": "session->s = h2_ctx_server_get(ctx);\nsession->config = h2_config_sget(session->s);\nsession->max_stream_count = h2_config_geti(session->config, H2_CONF_MAX_STREAMS);", "fixing_pattern": "Directly passing the server configuration settings as a parameter to the function, ensuring that the configuration is consistent and properly initialized.", "fixing_example": "session->s = s;\nsession->max_stream_count = h2_config_sgeti(s, H2_CONF_MAX_STREAMS);"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure Configuration Handling", "functionality": "The code handles configuration settings for server and proxy connections, including insecure settings.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Directly setting insecure configuration values without proper validation or access control.", "vulnerable_example": "cfg.set(\"server\", \"insecure\", \"1\")", "fixing_pattern": "Using a more secure configuration handling mechanism that ensures proper validation and access control.", "fixing_example": "conf[\"server\"][\"insecure\"] = \"1\""}
{"pattern_name": "Unrestricted Model Field Access", "functionality": "The code allows access to model fields without proper validation of whether the field represents a relationship between models.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code does not validate if a field is a relationship field before allowing access to it, potentially exposing sensitive information.", "vulnerable_example": "def get_field_value(model, field_name):\n    return getattr(model, field_name)", "fixing_pattern": "The fix involves adding validation to ensure that the field is a relationship field before allowing access to it.", "fixing_example": "def get_field_value(model, field_name):\n    field = model._meta.get_field(field_name)\n    if not field.is_relation:\n        raise ValueError('Field is not a relationship field')\n    return getattr(model, field_name)"}
{"pattern_name": "Missing CSRF Protection in HTTP POST Handler", "functionality": "The code handles an HTTP POST request to create a new blog post. It checks for authorization, creates a blog post, updates the editor with the new content, validates the model state, publishes the blog post, and redirects to the admin blog page.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The HTTP POST handler is vulnerable to Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) attacks because it lacks the `[ValidateAntiForgeryToken]` attribute. This attribute is necessary to ensure that the request originates from the same user session and not from a malicious third-party site.", "vulnerable_example": "[HttpPost, ActionName(\"Create\")]\npublic ActionResult CreatePOST() {\n    // Code to create a blog post\n}", "fixing_pattern": "To fix the vulnerability, the `[ValidateAntiForgeryToken]` attribute should be added to the HTTP POST handler. This attribute validates the anti-forgery token in the request, ensuring that the request is legitimate and not forged by an attacker.", "fixing_example": "[HttpPost, ActionName(\"Create\")]\n[ValidateAntiForgeryToken]\npublic ActionResult CreatePOST() {\n    // Code to create a blog post\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Missing CSRF Protection in HTTP POST Handler", "functionality": "The code handles an HTTP POST request to create a contact by validating the input model, checking for a required Job ID, loading a job from a repository, and returning a view with the job's simplified ID.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code is vulnerable to Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) attacks because it lacks the `[ValidateAntiForgeryToken]` attribute on the HTTP POST handler. This allows an attacker to forge requests on behalf of authenticated users, potentially changing application state without their consent.", "vulnerable_example": "[HttpPost]\npublic ActionResult DeleteAccount(int accountId)\n{\n    // Delete account logic\n    return RedirectToAction(\"Index\");\n}", "fixing_pattern": "To mitigate CSRF vulnerabilities, the `[ValidateAntiForgeryToken]` attribute should be added to the HTTP POST handler. This ensures that the request includes a valid anti-forgery token, which is verified by the server to confirm the request's authenticity.", "fixing_example": "[HttpPost]\n[ValidateAntiForgeryToken]\npublic ActionResult DeleteAccount(int accountId)\n{\n    // Delete account logic\n    return RedirectToAction(\"Index\");\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Unrestricted Application Property Routing", "functionality": "The code uses application properties to determine the routing expression for function execution. These properties can be used to dynamically construct URLs or other resources.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The routing expression from application properties is passed directly to a function without validation or restriction, allowing potential manipulation of the expression to bypass URL restrictions.", "vulnerable_example": "function = this.functionFromExpression(functionProperties.getRoutingExpression(), message);", "fixing_pattern": "The routing expression from application properties is now passed with an additional boolean parameter to enforce validation or restrictions, preventing unauthorized URL access.", "fixing_example": "function = this.functionFromExpression(functionProperties.getRoutingExpression(), message, false);"}
{"pattern_name": "Hardcoded Endpoint Path", "functionality": "The code defines a hardcoded endpoint path for the Cloud Foundry application, which is used to map and handle requests.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using a hardcoded string for the endpoint path, which can lead to security bypass if the path is predictable or known.", "vulnerable_example": "return new CloudFoundryWebFluxEndpointHandlerMapping(new EndpointMapping(\"/cloudfoundryapplication\"), webEndpoints, endpointMediaTypes, getCorsConfiguration(), securityInterceptor, new EndpointLinksResolver(allEndpoints));", "fixing_pattern": "Replacing the hardcoded endpoint path with a constant or configurable value to reduce predictability and improve security.", "fixing_example": "return new CloudFoundryWebFluxEndpointHandlerMapping(new EndpointMapping(BASE_PATH), webEndpoints, endpointMediaTypes, getCorsConfiguration(), securityInterceptor, allEndpoints);"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure Deserialization with Insufficient Type Validation", "functionality": "The code provides functionality to serialize and deserialize objects of allowed types. It uses a whitelist of allowed classes to restrict deserialization to specific types.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code attempts to restrict deserialization by checking the expected type against a whitelist of allowed classes. However, the deserialization process itself is insecure because it does not validate the actual deserialized object's type before casting it. This can lead to deserialization of malicious objects if the whitelist check is bypassed or if the deserialized object is not of the expected type.", "vulnerable_example": "public static <T extends Serializable> T deserializeObject(byte[] bytes, Class<T> expectedType) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {\n    if (bytes == null) {\n        throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Byte array to deserialize cannot be null\");\n    }\n\n    if (expectedType == null) {\n        throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Expected type cannot be null\");\n    }\n\n    if (!ALLOWED_CLASSES.contains(expectedType)) {\n        throw new SecurityException(\"Deserialization of type \" + expectedType.getName() + \" is not allowed\");\n    }\n\n    try (ByteArrayInputStream bais = new ByteArrayInputStream(bytes);\n         ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(bais)) {\n        Object obj = ois.readObject();\n        return expectedType.cast(obj);\n    }\n}", "fixing_pattern": "To fix the vulnerability, the deserialization process should include additional validation to ensure the deserialized object is of the expected type before casting. This can be achieved by explicitly checking the type of the deserialized object using `instanceof` or similar mechanisms.", "fixing_example": "public static <T extends Serializable> T deserializeObject(byte[] bytes, Class<T> expectedType) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {\n    if (bytes == null) {\n        throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Byte array to deserialize cannot be null\");\n    }\n\n    if (expectedType == null) {\n        throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Expected type cannot be null\");\n    }\n\n    if (!ALLOWED_CLASSES.contains(expectedType)) {\n        throw new SecurityException(\"Deserialization of type \" + expectedType.getName() + \" is not allowed\");\n    }\n\n    try (ByteArrayInputStream bais = new ByteArrayInputStream(bytes);\n         ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(bais)) {\n        Object obj = ois.readObject();\n        if (expectedType.isInstance(obj)) {\n            return expectedType.cast(obj);\n        } else {\n            throw new SecurityException(\"Deserialized object is not of the expected type\");\n        }\n    }\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Unsafe JSON Serialization with Direct String Output", "functionality": "The code serializes a Java object into a JSON string and directly returns the serialized string without any sanitization or encoding.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The vulnerable code uses a `StringWriter` to serialize a Java object into a JSON string and returns the raw string, which can include unescaped HTML or JavaScript content.", "vulnerable_example": "private <T> String serialize(T bean, Class<T> clazz) throws IOException {\n    StringWriter w = new StringWriter();\n    Model<T> model = new ModelBuilder().get(clazz);\n    model.writeTo(bean, Flavor.JSON.createDataWriter(bean, w, config));\n    return w.toString();\n}", "fixing_pattern": "The fix delegates the serialization to a separate method that likely includes proper sanitization or encoding of the JSON output to prevent XSS vulnerabilities.", "fixing_example": "private <T> String serialize(T bean, Class<T> clazz) throws IOException {\n    return serialize(bean, clazz, config);\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Incomplete Error Status Assignment", "functionality": "The code processes a request and handles errors by returning error codes, but doesn't always set the status message before returning.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Returning an error code without setting the status message, which can lead to incomplete error reporting and debugging difficulties.", "vulnerable_example": "if (code)\n    return code;", "fixing_pattern": "Ensuring that all error return paths set the appropriate status message before returning.", "fixing_example": "if (code) {\n    *status = \"DECODE_PA_FOR_USER\";\n    return code;\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Unrestricted HTTP Method Handling", "functionality": "The code processes incoming HTTP requests without properly validating the HTTP method, allowing any method to be handled.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code assumes the request method is 'GET' using an assertion, which does not enforce any restrictions or handle other methods gracefully.", "vulnerable_example": "assert job.requestMethod() == b'GET'", "fixing_pattern": "The code now explicitly checks if the request method is 'GET' and fails the request if it is not, preventing the handling of potentially dangerous methods like 'POST'.", "fixing_example": "if job.requestMethod() != b'GET':\n    job.fail(QWebEngineUrlRequestJob.RequestDenied)\n    return"}
{"pattern_name": "Insufficient Network Access Control", "functionality": "The code creates a network port configuration without properly restricting allowed IP address pairs for communication.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Creating network port configurations without enforcing IP address restrictions, potentially allowing unauthorized network access.", "vulnerable_example": "ovs_port = mock.Mock(vif_mac='00:00:00:00:00:00', ofport=1,\n                         port_name=\"port-name\")", "fixing_pattern": "Implementing IP address restrictions by adding allowed IP pairs for both IPv4 and IPv6 to the port configuration.", "fixing_example": "allowed_pairs_v4 = ovsfw.OFPort._get_allowed_pairs(\n        port_dict, version=constants.IPv4)\n    allowed_pairs_v6 = ovsfw.OFPort._get_allowed_pairs(\n        port_dict, version=constants.IPv6)\n    ovs_port = mock.Mock(vif_mac='00:00:00:00:00:00', ofport=1,\n                         port_name=\"port-name\",\n                         allowed_pairs_v4=allowed_pairs_v4,\n                         allowed_pairs_v6=allowed_pairs_v6)"}
{"pattern_name": "Unvalidated Port Binding in Server Setup", "functionality": "The code sets up a ZooKeeper server by creating necessary directories, configuring transaction logs, and binding to a port within a specified range to establish a connection factory.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code directly uses user-provided port values to create an InetSocketAddress without validating or sanitizing the input. This can lead to Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) if an attacker supplies malicious port values, potentially redirecting or manipulating the server's network requests.", "vulnerable_example": "InetSocketAddress address = new InetSocketAddress(userProvidedPort);", "fixing_pattern": "To mitigate SSRF, validate the port range against a predefined list of allowed ports or ensure the port values are within a safe and expected range. Additionally, restrict the use of user input in network-related operations.", "fixing_example": "int[] allowedPorts = {2800, 2801, 2802, 2900};\nif (!Arrays.asList(allowedPorts).contains(port)) {\n    throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Invalid port specified\");\n}\nInetSocketAddress address = new InetSocketAddress(port);"}
{"pattern_name": "Inefficient Authentication Check on Main Event Loop", "functionality": "The code checks user permissions for accessing an API endpoint by iterating through the required permissions and verifying authorization for each one. This check is performed synchronously on the main event loop.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The authorization check is performed on the main event loop, which can block other requests and lead to a denial of service if the check is computationally expensive or if there are many invalid requests.", "vulnerable_example": "if (authenticationService.isPresent()) {\n  if (optionalUser.isPresent()) {\n    User user = optionalUser.get();\n    for (String perm : jsonRpcMethod.getPermissions()) {\n      user.isAuthorized(\n          perm,\n          (authed) -> {\n            if (authed.result()) {\n              foundMatchingPermission.set(true);\n            }\n          });\n    }\n  }\n}", "fixing_pattern": "The fix optimizes the permission check by returning early if a matching permission is found, reducing unnecessary iterations. Additionally, it simplifies the logic by handling the case where no authentication service is present separately.", "fixing_example": "if (authenticationService.isEmpty()) {\n  return true;\n}\nif (optionalUser.isPresent()) {\n  User user = optionalUser.get();\n  for (String perm : jsonRpcMethod.getPermissions()) {\n    user.isAuthorized(\n        perm,\n        (authed) -> {\n          if (authed.result()) {\n            foundMatchingPermission.set(true);\n          }\n        });\n    if (foundMatchingPermission.get()) {\n      return foundMatchingPermission.get();\n    }\n  }\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Hardcoded API Key", "functionality": "The code defines constants for various API keys and URLs used in the application, including a Google Maps API key, to facilitate integration with external services.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The vulnerability arises from hardcoding sensitive information, such as API keys, directly in the source code. This practice exposes the keys to anyone with access to the codebase, making it easier for attackers to misuse them.", "vulnerable_example": "define('LL_STRIPE_KEY', 'sk_test_51JkLZ2SJmQ9WVdrOVREWTJXVnBFTXpBbWNHbzlNQS0tJnM9Y29uc3VtZXJzZWNyZXQmeD02Mw');", "fixing_pattern": "To resolve this issue, sensitive information like API keys should be stored in environment variables or secure configuration files outside the codebase. This ensures that the keys are not exposed in the source code and can be managed securely.", "fixing_example": "define('LL_GMAPS_KEY', getenv('GMAPS_API_KEY'));"}
