{"pattern_name": "Unsafe Configuration Handling in HTTP/2 Upgrade", "functionality": "The code handles configuration settings for HTTP/2, specifically the number of workers, by querying and setting minimum and maximum worker values based on server configuration.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code does not properly validate or handle the configuration settings for HTTP/2 workers, leading to potential misconfiguration when handling HTTP/1.1 to HTTP/2 upgrade requests.", "vulnerable_example": "void h2_get_num_workers(server_rec *s, int *minw, int *maxw) {\n    int threads_per_child = 0;\n    const h2_config *config = h2_config_sget(s);\n    *minw = h2_config_geti(config, H2_CONF_MIN_WORKERS);\n    *maxw = h2_config_geti(config, H2_CONF_MAX_WORKERS);    \n    ap_mpm_query(AP_MPMQ_MAX_THREADS, &threads_per_child);\n    if (*minw <= 0) {\n        *minw = threads_per_child;\n    }\n    if (*maxw <= 0) {\n        *maxw = 3 * (*minw) / 2;\n        if (*maxw < 4) {\n            *maxw = 4;\n        }\n    }\n}", "fixing_pattern": "The fix introduces a new function to handle the configuration of early hints in HTTP/2, ensuring proper validation and logging of configuration values. This prevents misconfiguration by enforcing valid values and logging warnings for invalid settings.", "fixing_example": "static const char *h2_conf_set_early_hints(cmd_parms *cmd,\n                                           void *dirconf, const char *value) {\n    int val;\n    if (!strcasecmp(value, \"On\")) val = 1;\n    else if (!strcasecmp(value, \"Off\")) val = 0;\n    else return \"value must be On or Off\";\n    CONFIG_CMD_SET(cmd, dirconf, H2_CONF_EARLY_HINTS, val);\n    if (cmd->path) {\n        ap_log_perror(APLOG_MARK, APLOG_WARNING, 0, cmd->pool, \n                            \"H2EarlyHints = %d on path %s\", val, cmd->path);\n    }\n    return NULL;\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Unvalidated Port Binding in Server Setup", "functionality": "The code sets up a ZooKeeper server by creating necessary directories, configuring transaction logs, and binding to a port within a specified range to establish a connection factory.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code directly uses user-provided port values to create an InetSocketAddress without validating or sanitizing the input. This can lead to Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) if an attacker supplies malicious port values, potentially redirecting or manipulating the server's network requests.", "vulnerable_example": "InetSocketAddress address = new InetSocketAddress(userProvidedPort);", "fixing_pattern": "To mitigate SSRF, validate the port range against a predefined list of allowed ports or ensure the port values are within a safe and expected range. Additionally, restrict the use of user input in network-related operations.", "fixing_example": "int[] allowedPorts = {2800, 2801, 2802, 2900};\nif (!Arrays.asList(allowedPorts).contains(port)) {\n    throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Invalid port specified\");\n}\nInetSocketAddress address = new InetSocketAddress(port);"}
