{"pattern_name": "Incomplete Resource Cleanup", "functionality": "The code handles HTTP requests and manages resources like memory and brigade objects during request processing.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Missing cleanup of brigade resources before reusing them, which could lead to resource leaks or undefined behavior.", "vulnerable_example": "The code processes HTTP requests and uses brigade objects but doesn't clean them up before reusing them in certain code paths.", "fixing_pattern": "Adding explicit cleanup of brigade resources before reusing them to prevent resource leaks and ensure proper memory management.", "fixing_example": "if (!parent_req) {\n  php_apache_request_dtor(r TSRMLS_CC);\n  ctx->request_processed = 1;\n  apr_brigade_cleanup(brigade);\n  bucket = apr_bucket_eos_create(r->connection->bucket_alloc);\n  APR_BRIGADE_INSERT_TAIL(brigade, bucket);\n  rv = ap_pass_brigade(r->output_filters, brigade);\n  if (rv != APR_SUCCESS || r->connection->aborted) {\n    zend_first_try {\n      php_handle_aborted_connection();\n    } zend_end_try();\n  }\n  apr_brigade_cleanup(brigade);\n  apr_pool_cleanup_run(r->pool, (void *)&SG(server_context), php_server_context_cleanup);\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Inadequate Query Parameter Handling", "functionality": "The code processes and validates query parameters in HTTP requests.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The query parameter handling logic does not properly decode URL-encoded spaces, leading to potential misinterpretation of query parameters.", "vulnerable_example": "PathAndQuery.parse(\"/a?b=c\")", "fixing_pattern": "The fix includes updating the query parameter handling logic to correctly decode URL-encoded spaces (`%20`), ensuring that query parameters are accurately interpreted.", "fixing_example": "PathAndQuery.parse(\"/%20?%20\")"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure Parameter Handling in Test Cases", "functionality": "The test cases handle parameters that could be manipulated to execute arbitrary code.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The test cases include parameters that are not properly sanitized, making them susceptible to injection attacks.", "vulnerable_example": "assertPlotParam(\"smooth\", \"unwrap\");", "fixing_pattern": "The fix involves updating the test cases to handle parameters securely by adding validation and sanitization checks.", "fixing_example": "assertInvalidPlotParam(\"fgcolor\", \"%5BDEADBE\");"}
{"pattern_name": "Insufficient Session Cleanup", "functionality": "The code is responsible for cleaning up resources after test execution, but fails to properly terminate or invalidate active sessions.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The cleanup process only removes test case files but doesn't handle session termination or database cleanup, leaving active sessions and database connections potentially accessible.", "vulnerable_example": "def tearDown(self):\n    if hasattr(self, 'testcases'):\n        shutil.rmtree(self.testcases)\n        delattr(self, 'testcases')", "fixing_pattern": "Added explicit session termination and database cleanup by dropping all database sessions using cherrypy.tools.db.drop_all() to ensure proper session invalidation and resource cleanup.", "fixing_example": "def tearDown(self):\n    if hasattr(self, 'testcases'):\n        shutil.rmtree(self.testcases)\n        delattr(self, 'testcases')\n    cherrypy.tools.db.drop_all()"}
{"pattern_name": "Insufficient Logging of Sensitive Information", "functionality": "The code logs operation results but does not include detailed error or result text, which could lead to insufficient information for debugging or auditing.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The logging function does not include the result text from the operation block, potentially omitting critical information.", "vulnerable_example": "slapi_log_access( LDAP_DEBUG_STATS,\n  \"conn=%\" NSPRIu64 \" op=%d RESULT err=%d\"\n  \" tag=%\" BERTAG_T \" nentries=%d etime=%s%s%s\n\",\n  op->o_connid,\n  op->o_opid,\n  err, tag, nentries,\n  etime,\n  notes_str, csn_str );", "fixing_pattern": "The fix adds the retrieval and logging of the result text from the operation block, ensuring that detailed information is included in the logs.", "fixing_example": "char *pbtxt = NULL;\nchar *ext_str = NULL;\nslapi_pblock_get(pb, SLAPI_PB_RESULT_TEXT, &pbtxt);\nif (pbtxt) {\n  ext_str = slapi_ch_smprintf(\" - %s\", pbtxt);\n} else {\n  ext_str = \"\";\n}\nslapi_log_access( LDAP_DEBUG_STATS,\n  \"conn=%\" NSPRIu64 \" op=%d RESULT err=%d\"\n  \" tag=%\" BERTAG_T \" nentries=%d etime=%s%s%s%s\n\",\n  op->o_connid,\n  op->o_opid,\n  err, tag, nentries,\n  etime,\n  notes_str, csn_str, ext_str);\nif (pbtxt) {\n  slapi_ch_free_string(&ext_str);\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure File Handling", "functionality": "The code writes source code to a file and then compiles it. The file handling does not ensure proper cleanup in case of exceptions.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Opening a file and writing to it without ensuring it is properly closed in case of exceptions, which can lead to resource leaks or incomplete cleanup.", "vulnerable_example": "f = open(fname, 'w')\nf.write(source)\nf.close()", "fixing_pattern": "Using a `try-finally` block to ensure the file is properly closed even if an exception occurs during the write operation.", "fixing_example": "try:\n    f.write(source)\n    f.flush()\nfinally:\n    f.close()"}
{"pattern_name": "Missing CSRF Protection", "functionality": "The code handles an HTTP POST request to create a new thread and post in a forum, updating the content and publishing it after validation.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code lacks the `[ValidateAntiForgeryToken]` attribute on the HTTP POST method handler, making it vulnerable to Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) attacks. CSRF allows an attacker to trick a user into performing unintended actions on a web application in which they are authenticated.", "vulnerable_example": "[HttpPost, ActionName(\"Create\")]\npublic ActionResult CreatePOST(int forumId) {\n    // Code to create and publish thread and post\n    return Redirect(redirectUrl);\n}", "fixing_pattern": "To mitigate CSRF, the `[ValidateAntiForgeryToken]` attribute should be added to the HTTP POST method handler. This ensures that the request includes a valid anti-forgery token, which is verified by the server to confirm the request's authenticity.", "fixing_example": "[HttpPost, ActionName(\"Create\")]\n[ValidateAntiForgeryToken]\npublic ActionResult CreatePOST(int forumId) {\n    // Code to create and publish thread and post\n    return Redirect(redirectUrl);\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Unsafe API Token Initialization", "functionality": "The code initializes an API token for a service without proper validation or type enforcement.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The function accepts a generic 'bot' parameter without type checking, which could lead to improper initialization of API tokens if an incorrect object is passed.", "vulnerable_example": "async def initialize(self, bot):\n    key = await self.config.tenorkey()\n    if not key:\n        return\n    await bot.set_shared_api_tokens(\"tenor\", api_key=key)\n    await self.config.tenorkey.clear()", "fixing_pattern": "The fix adds type enforcement by specifying the expected type of the 'bot' parameter, ensuring that only objects of the correct type can be passed to the function.", "fixing_example": "async def initialize(self, bot: bot.Red):\n    key = await self.config.tenorkey()\n    if not key:\n        return\n    await bot.set_shared_api_tokens(\"tenor\", api_key=key)\n    await self.config.tenorkey.clear()"}
{"pattern_name": "Hardcoded API Key in Firebase Configuration", "functionality": "The code initializes a Firebase application using a configuration object that includes an API key. This setup allows the application to interact with Firebase services such as the Realtime Database.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The API key is hardcoded directly in the source code, which is an unsafe practice. Hardcoding sensitive credentials like API keys exposes them to potential misuse if the code is shared, leaked, or reverse-engineered. This violates the principle of keeping secrets out of source code.", "vulnerable_example": "const firebaseConfig = {\n    apiKey: 'AIzaSyB3ZvJDuZ2HD-UppgPvY2by-GI0KnessXw',\n    authDomain: 'rlexa-9f1ca.firebaseapp.com',\n    databaseURL: 'https://rlexa-9f1ca.firebaseio.com',\n    projectId: 'rlexa-9f1ca',\n    storageBucket: 'rlexa-9f1ca.appspot.com',\n    messagingSenderId: '161670508523'\n};", "fixing_pattern": "The API key should be stored in a secure environment variable or a secrets management service, and accessed dynamically at runtime. This ensures that sensitive credentials are not exposed in the source code and can be managed securely.", "fixing_example": "const firebaseConfig = {\n    apiKey: process.env.FIREBASE_API_KEY,\n    authDomain: 'rlexa-9f1ca.firebaseapp.com',\n    databaseURL: 'https://rlexa-9f1ca.firebaseio.com',\n    projectId: 'rlexa-9f1ca',\n    storageBucket: 'rlexa-9f1ca.appspot.com',\n    messagingSenderId: '161670508523'\n};"}
{"pattern_name": "Hardcoded Credentials in Configuration", "functionality": "The code retrieves database configuration settings based on the environment (CI or default). It dynamically selects the database, user, and password based on whether the CI environment variable is set to 'true'.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code hardcodes sensitive credentials (passwords) directly in the source code. This is a common insecure practice that exposes credentials to anyone with access to the codebase, making it easier for attackers to exploit.", "vulnerable_example": "def get_database_config():\n    if os.environ.get('CI') == 'true':\n        password = 'ci_password'\n    else:\n        password = 'default_password'\n    return {'password': password}", "fixing_pattern": "To resolve this vulnerability, sensitive credentials should be stored securely using a secret management system (e.g., environment variables, vaults, or cloud-based secret managers). The code should retrieve credentials from these secure sources instead of hardcoding them.", "fixing_example": "def get_database_config():\n    password = os.environ.get('DB_PASSWORD', 'fallback_password')\n    return {'password': password}"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure File Permission Assignment", "functionality": "The code creates a database file and sets its permissions after the file has been created and used.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Setting file permissions after the file has been created and used, which leaves the file with potentially insecure default permissions during its initial creation and usage.", "vulnerable_example": "conn = self._connect()\ntry:\n    with conn:\n        self._create_tables(conn, last_upgrade_to_run)\nfinally:\n    conn.close()\nos.chmod(self.filename, stat.S_IRUSR | stat.S_IWUSR)", "fixing_pattern": "Setting secure file permissions at the time of file creation using os.open() with appropriate flags and mode, ensuring the file is never created with insecure permissions.", "fixing_example": "fd = os.open(\n    self.filename,\n    flags=os.O_RDWR | os.O_CREAT,\n    mode=stat.S_IRUSR | stat.S_IWUSR,\n)\nos.close(fd)\nconn = self._connect()\ntry:\n    with conn:\n        self._create_tables(conn, last_upgrade_to_run)\nfinally:\n    conn.close()"}
{"pattern_name": "Uninitialized Structure Field", "functionality": "The code constructs a message structure to be broadcasted, but fails to initialize all fields of the structure.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Creating a structure and not initializing all its fields, potentially leaving sensitive memory areas uninitialized.", "vulnerable_example": "hdr->sadb_msg_len = (sizeof(struct sadb_msg) / sizeof(uint64_t));\n// Missing initialization of sadb_msg_reserved field", "fixing_pattern": "Explicitly initializing all fields of the structure, including reserved fields, to prevent potential memory leaks or undefined behavior.", "fixing_example": "hdr->sadb_msg_len = (sizeof(struct sadb_msg) / sizeof(uint64_t));\nhdr->sadb_msg_reserved = 0;"}
{"pattern_name": "Incorrect Memory Allocation Flag Usage", "functionality": "The code determines the appropriate memory allocation flags for huge pages based on their support for migration.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using an incorrect condition to check for memory allocation flags, potentially leading to improper memory handling and reference count overflow.", "vulnerable_example": "if (hugepage_migration_supported(h))\n    return GFP_HIGHUSER_MOVABLE;\nelse\n    return GFP_HIGHUSER;", "fixing_pattern": "Replacing the incorrect condition with the correct one to ensure proper memory allocation flags are used, preventing reference count overflow.", "fixing_example": "if (hugepage_movable_supported(h))\n    return GFP_HIGHUSER_MOVABLE;\nelse\n    return GFP_HIGHUSER;"}
{"pattern_name": "SQL Injection via String Concatenation", "functionality": "The code performs CRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete) operations on a SQLite database by dynamically constructing SQL queries based on user-provided inputs.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The vulnerability arises from directly concatenating user-provided inputs (such as table names, conditions, and data) into SQL query strings. This allows an attacker to inject malicious SQL code, leading to unauthorized database access or manipulation.", "vulnerable_example": "query = f\"SELECT * FROM {table} WHERE {condition}\"\ncursor.execute(query)", "fixing_pattern": "To prevent SQL injection, use parameterized queries or prepared statements. This ensures that user inputs are treated as data rather than executable code. Additionally, validate and sanitize inputs to ensure they conform to expected formats.", "fixing_example": "query = \"SELECT * FROM ? WHERE ?\"\ncursor.execute(query, (table, condition))"}
{"pattern_name": "Insufficient Sibling Validation", "functionality": "The code attempts to add a route to a node in the routing table, checking if the route has siblings.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code does not validate if the sibling route qualifies for ECMP before adding it to the sibling list, potentially leading to incorrect routing behavior.", "vulnerable_example": "if (sibling->rt6i_metric == rt->rt6i_metric) {\n    list_add_tail(&rt->rt6i_siblings,\n                  &sibling->rt6i_siblings);\n    break;\n}", "fixing_pattern": "The fix introduces a validation function `rt6_qualify_for_ecmp` to ensure that the sibling route qualifies for ECMP before adding it to the sibling list.", "fixing_example": "if (sibling->rt6i_metric == rt->rt6i_metric &&\n    rt6_qualify_for_ecmp(sibling)) {\n    list_add_tail(&rt->rt6i_siblings,\n                  &sibling->rt6i_siblings);\n    break;\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Incomplete Error Handling", "functionality": "The code processes various HTTP methods (e.g., PUT, DELETE) and performs corresponding actions based on the method.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code lacks proper error handling for certain conditions, such as when a URI is not registered, which could lead to undefined behavior or security issues.", "vulnerable_example": "} else\nif (strstr(path, HTPASSWD)) {", "fixing_pattern": "The code adds a default error handling case that sends a '403 Forbidden' response when the URI is not registered, ensuring that all cases are properly handled.", "fixing_example": "} else {\n\t_shttpd_send_server_error(c, 403, \"Forbidden\");\n}"}
