{"pattern_name": "Insufficient Validation of Input Data in Control Flow", "functionality": "The code processes input data (MSR values) and performs operations based on the input. It includes a control flow check to determine whether certain operations should be executed.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code fails to validate the state of a critical resource (synic->active) before performing operations that depend on it. This can lead to unsafe operations being executed when the resource is not in a valid state.", "vulnerable_example": "case HV_X64_MSR_EOM: {\n\tint i;\n\tfor (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(synic->sint); i++)\n\t\tkvm_hv_notify_acked_sint(vcpu, i);\n\tbreak;\n}", "fixing_pattern": "The fix adds a validation check to ensure the resource (synic->active) is in a valid state before proceeding with the operation. This prevents unsafe operations when the resource is not active.", "fixing_example": "case HV_X64_MSR_EOM: {\n\tint i;\n\tif (!synic->active)\n\t\tbreak;\n\tfor (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(synic->sint); i++)\n\t\tkvm_hv_notify_acked_sint(vcpu, i);\n\tbreak;\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Unconditional Resource Deallocation", "functionality": "The code allocates a dynamic context for GIF image processing, processes the image, extracts the data, and then unconditionally frees the context.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code frees the allocated context (`out`) unconditionally, regardless of whether the image processing (`gdImageGifCtx`) succeeds or fails. This can lead to a double-free vulnerability if the context is already freed or if the processing fails.", "vulnerable_example": "void * gdImageGifPtr (gdImagePtr im, int *size)\n{\n  void *rv;\n  gdIOCtx *out = gdNewDynamicCtx (2048, NULL);\n  gdImageGifCtx (im, out);\n  rv = gdDPExtractData (out, size);\n  out->gd_free (out);\n  return rv;\n}", "fixing_pattern": "The fix introduces a conditional check to ensure the context is only freed if the image processing succeeds. If the processing fails, the context is not freed, preventing a double-free scenario.", "fixing_example": "void * gdImageGifPtr (gdImagePtr im, int *size)\n{\n  void *rv;\n  gdIOCtx *out = gdNewDynamicCtx (2048, NULL);\n  if (!_gdImageGifCtx(im, out)) {\n    rv = gdDPExtractData(out, size);\n  } else {\n    rv = NULL;\n  }\n  out->gd_free (out);\n  return rv;\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Unrestricted File Upload Without Validation", "functionality": "The code processes file uploads by retrieving file paths from a request and directly fetching the files from storage without validating the file type or origin.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code accepts file paths from user input and directly fetches the files from storage without any validation or verification of the file's safety or origin.", "vulnerable_example": "request.FILES.setlist(field_name, list(self.get_files_from_storage(paths)))", "fixing_pattern": "The fix introduces validation by requiring a signature for the file upload and checking for suspicious file operations. This ensures that only authorized and safe files are processed.", "fixing_example": "request.FILES.setlist(field_name, list(self.get_files_from_storage(paths, signature)))"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure Temporary File Path Handling", "functionality": "The code processes file uploads and handles temporary file paths, including sanitizing file names to ensure they are safe for use in the filesystem.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code does not properly handle spaces in temporary file paths, which can lead to misinterpretation of the path and unintended file operations (e.g., deletion).", "vulnerable_example": "file_name = force_text(file_name, encoding, errors='replace')\nfile_name = self.sanitize_file_name(file_name)", "fixing_pattern": "The fix ensures that spaces in temporary file paths are properly handled by adding additional checks and safeguards to prevent misinterpretation of the path.", "fixing_example": "file_name = force_text(file_name, encoding, errors='replace')\nfile_name = self.sanitize_file_name(file_name)\n# Additional checks and safeguards are added to handle spaces in paths"}
{"pattern_name": "Inconsistent Error Handling", "functionality": "The code handles errors by returning early with a status code, but the error handling is inconsistent and does not always clean up resources properly.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Error handling is scattered and inconsistent, with some paths returning early without cleaning up resources or using inconsistent return values.", "vulnerable_example": "if ( num2-1 != 0 ) { fprintf(stderr, ...); free(argbuf); return(-1); }", "fixing_pattern": "The fix consolidates error handling by using a `goto EXIT` pattern, ensuring consistent cleanup and error handling across all paths.", "fixing_example": "if ( num2-1 != 0 ) { fprintf(stderr, ...); goto EXIT; }"}
{"pattern_name": "Incomplete Error Status Assignment", "functionality": "The code processes a request and handles errors by returning error codes, but doesn't always set the status message before returning.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Returning an error code without setting the status message, which can lead to incomplete error reporting and debugging difficulties.", "vulnerable_example": "if (code)\n    return code;", "fixing_pattern": "Ensuring that all error return paths set the appropriate status message before returning.", "fixing_example": "if (code) {\n    *status = \"DECODE_PA_FOR_USER\";\n    return code;\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure Cloud Foundry Environment Variable Handling", "functionality": "The application uses environment variables provided by Cloud Foundry to determine security configurations or access controls.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The application trusts environment variables without proper validation or sanitization, potentially allowing an attacker to manipulate these variables to bypass security checks.", "vulnerable_example": "String cloudFoundryEnv = System.getenv(\"CLOUD_FOUNDRY_ENV\");\nif (cloudFoundryEnv != null && cloudFoundryEnv.equals(\"production\")) {\n    // Bypass security checks\n}", "fixing_pattern": "The fix involves validating and sanitizing environment variables before using them in security-critical decisions. Additionally, the application should enforce strict checks and not rely solely on environment variables for security configurations.", "fixing_example": "String cloudFoundryEnv = System.getenv(\"CLOUD_FOUNDRY_ENV\");\nif (cloudFoundryEnv != null && cloudFoundryEnv.equals(\"production\")) {\n    // Perform additional security checks\n    if (isValidEnvironment(cloudFoundryEnv)) {\n        // Proceed with secure operations\n    }\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure XML External Entity (XXE) Processing", "functionality": "The code loads and updates a configuration file stored in a secure directory. It reads the file using an XML parser, applies updates to the XML document, and saves the changes atomically.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The XML parser is configured to prohibit DTD processing and disable the XML resolver, but the `XmlReader` settings are not explicitly enforced during the `Load` operation. This could allow an attacker to exploit XML External Entity (XXE) processing if the parser inadvertently processes maliciously crafted XML input.", "vulnerable_example": "XmlDocument configurationFile = new XmlDocument();\nusing (XmlReader reader = XmlReader.Create(configFilePath))\n{\n    configurationFile.Load(reader);\n}", "fixing_pattern": "To prevent XXE vulnerabilities, ensure the `XmlReader` settings are explicitly enforced during the `Load` operation. Use a secure `XmlReader` instance with DTD processing prohibited and the XML resolver disabled.", "fixing_example": "XmlDocument configurationFile = new XmlDocument();\nXmlReaderSettings settings = new XmlReaderSettings\n{\n    DtdProcessing = DtdProcessing.Prohibit,\n    XmlResolver = null\n};\nusing (XmlReader reader = XmlReader.Create(configFilePath, settings))\n{\n    configurationFile.Load(reader);\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure Parameter Handling in Test Cases", "functionality": "The test cases handle parameters that could be manipulated to execute arbitrary code.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The test cases include parameters that are not properly sanitized, making them susceptible to injection attacks.", "vulnerable_example": "assertPlotParam(\"smooth\", \"unwrap\");", "fixing_pattern": "The fix involves updating the test cases to handle parameters securely by adding validation and sanitization checks.", "fixing_example": "assertInvalidPlotParam(\"fgcolor\", \"%5BDEADBE\");"}
{"pattern_name": "Hardcoded Credentials in Test Code", "functionality": "The code tests the login functionality of a web application by sending HTTP POST requests with both invalid and valid credentials to a login endpoint. It then checks the response for expected error messages or successful login indicators.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code hardcodes sensitive credentials (usernames and passwords) directly in the source code. This practice is insecure because it exposes credentials to anyone with access to the codebase, making it easier for attackers to exploit them.", "vulnerable_example": "def test_login_functionality():\n    username = 'admin'\n    password = 'admin123'\n    response = requests.post('https://example.com/login', data={'username': username, 'password': password})", "fixing_pattern": "To resolve this issue, credentials should be stored securely using a secrets management system or environment variables. This ensures that sensitive information is not embedded in the code and can be managed securely.", "fixing_example": "import os\n\ndef test_login_functionality():\n    username = os.getenv('TEST_USERNAME')\n    password = os.getenv('TEST_PASSWORD')\n    response = requests.post('https://example.com/login', data={'username': username, 'password': password})"}
{"pattern_name": "Unrestricted Image Format Processing", "functionality": "The code processes an uploaded image file without restricting the allowed image formats.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using Image.open() without specifying allowed formats, which could potentially allow processing of unsafe or unexpected image formats.", "vulnerable_example": "image = Image.open(file)", "fixing_pattern": "Restricting image processing to specific, safe formats by using the formats parameter in Image.open().", "fixing_example": "image = Image.open(file, formats=settings.PILLOW_FORMATS_QUESTIONS_IMAGE)"}
{"pattern_name": "Incomplete Access Control for API Endpoints", "functionality": "The code generates links to API endpoints without ensuring that the user has the necessary permissions to access those endpoints.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code generates links to API endpoints (e.g., for inserting or dropping tables) without verifying if the user has the required permissions.", "vulnerable_example": "table_links.append({'path': self.ds.urls.table(name, table) + '/-/drop', 'label': 'Drop table ' + table, 'json': {'confirm': False}, 'method': 'POST'})", "fixing_pattern": "The code now explicitly checks if the user has the necessary permissions (e.g., 'insert-row', 'drop-table') before generating links to sensitive API endpoints.", "fixing_example": "if await self.ds.permission_allowed(request.actor, 'drop-table', (name, table)): table_links.append({'path': self.ds.urls.table(name, table) + '/-/drop', 'label': 'Drop table ' + table, 'json': {'confirm': False}, 'method': 'POST'})"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure Session Management in Database Initialization", "functionality": "The code initializes and configures a database connection, including setting up logging for debugging purposes.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The method initializes a database connection and sets up logging without ensuring proper session expiration or secure session handling.", "vulnerable_example": "def create_all(self):\n    self.on_end_resource()\n    base = self.get_base()\n    if base.metadata.bind is None:\n        dburi = cherrypy.config.get('tools.db.uri')\n        debug = cherrypy.config.get('tools.db.debug')\n        base.metadata.bind = create_engine(dburi)\n        if debug:\n            logging.getLogger('sqlalchemy.engine').setLevel(logging.DEBUG)\n    base.metadata.create_all()", "fixing_pattern": "The fix simplifies the method to only flush the session, removing the insecure initialization and configuration of the database connection.", "fixing_example": "def flush(self):\n    self.__class__.session.flush()\n    return self"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure Permission Control with OR Condition", "functionality": "The API endpoint checks permissions using an OR condition between multiple permission classes, allowing access if any one of them is satisfied.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using an OR condition (`|`) between permission classes in a way that allows unauthorized access if any single permission check passes.", "vulnerable_example": "permission_classes = [RBACPermission | IsSessionAssignee]", "fixing_pattern": "Replace the OR condition with a more restrictive permission check that requires all necessary conditions to be met, or implement proper authentication checks.", "fixing_example": "permission_classes = [IsAuthenticated, RBACPermission, IsSessionAssignee]"}
{"pattern_name": "Missing CSRF Token Validation", "functionality": "The code processes a request to create or modify a resource without validating the presence or correctness of a CSRF token.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The vulnerable code does not include a CSRF token in the request parameters, making it susceptible to CSRF attacks.", "vulnerable_example": "when(mockRequest.getParameter(\"templateprovider\")).thenReturn(templateProviderFullName);\nmockExistingTemplateProviders(templateProviderFullName,\n    new DocumentReference(\"xwiki\", Arrays.asList(\"XWiki\"), \"MyTemplateProvider\"), Collections.emptyList());", "fixing_pattern": "The fix involves adding a CSRF token to the request parameters to ensure that the request is legitimate and not forged.", "fixing_example": "verify(mockURLFactory).createURL(\"X\\\\.Y\", \"WebHome\", \"edit\",\n    \"template=&parent=Main.WebHome&title=X.Y&form_token=\" + CSRF_TOKEN_VALUE, null,\n    \"xwiki\", context);"}
{"pattern_name": "Missing Control Object Validation", "functionality": "The code attempts to create and use control objects without verifying their successful creation.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using control objects without checking if they were successfully created.", "vulnerable_example": "ControlObjectClient control = ControlObjectClient_create(\"simpleIOGenericIO/GGIO1.SPCSO1\", con);\n// Immediately using 'control' without checking if it's valid", "fixing_pattern": "Adding validation checks to ensure control objects are successfully created before using them.", "fixing_example": "ControlObjectClient control = ControlObjectClient_create(\"simpleIOGenericIO/GGIO1.SPCSO1\", con);\nif (control) {\n    // Safe to use 'control'\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure File Path Construction", "functionality": "The code constructs a file path dynamically using user-controlled input to generate an avatar image file.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The file path is constructed by directly concatenating user-controlled input (encoded) with a base directory path, which can lead to directory traversal or unauthorized file access.", "vulnerable_example": "File avatarFile = new File(Bootstrap.getSiteDir(), \"avatars/generated/\" + encoded + \".png\");", "fixing_pattern": "The file path is modified to include a more secure subdirectory ('assets') to restrict access and prevent directory traversal attacks.", "fixing_example": "File avatarFile = new File(Bootstrap.getSiteDir(), \"assets/avatars/generated/\" + encoded + \".png\");"}
{"pattern_name": "Insufficient Access Control in Filesystem Mounting", "functionality": "The code is responsible for handling the mounting of filesystems and ensuring proper access control to hidden files.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The original code lacks proper validation and access control checks when mounting filesystems, allowing unauthorized access to hidden files.", "vulnerable_example": "static bool has_locked_children(struct mount *mnt, struct dentry *dentry)\n{\n\tstruct mount *child;\n\tlist_for_each_entry(child, &mnt->mnt_mounts, mnt_child) {\n\t\tif (!is_subdir(child->mnt_mountpoint, dentry))\n\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\tif (child->mnt.mnt_flags & MNT_LOCKED)\n\t\t\treturn true;\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}", "fixing_pattern": "The fix introduces additional validation and access control checks, including verifying the mount point and ensuring proper propagation type for the mount operation.", "fixing_example": "static int do_change_type(struct path *path, int ms_flags)\n{\n\tstruct mount *m;\n\tstruct mount *mnt = real_mount(path->mnt);\n\tint recurse = ms_flags & MS_REC;\n\tint type;\n\tint err = 0;\n\tif (path->dentry != path->mnt->mnt_root)\n\t\treturn -EINVAL;\n\ttype = flags_to_propagation_type(ms_flags);\n\tif (!type)\n\t\treturn -EINVAL;\n\tnamespace_lock();\n\tif (type == MS_SHARED) {\n\t\terr = invent_group_ids(mnt, recurse);\n\t\tif (err)\n\t\t\tgoto out_unlock;\n\t}\n\tlock_mount_hash();\n\tfor (m = mnt; m; m = (recurse ? next_mnt(m, mnt) : NULL))\n\t\tchange_mnt_propagation(m, type);\n\tunlock_mount_hash();\n out_unlock:\n\tnamespace_unlock();\n\treturn err;\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Unvalidated Input Leading to Buffer Overflow", "functionality": "The code processes a GIF image by reading and interpreting LZW (Lempel-Ziv-Welch) compressed data. It uses the LZW code size to initialize the decompression process.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code does not validate the LZW code size (`lzw_cs`) before using it to calculate the `clear` value, which could lead to a buffer overflow if the code size is too large.", "vulnerable_example": "lzw_cs = gif_get8(s);\nclear = 1 << lzw_cs;", "fixing_pattern": "The fix adds a validation check to ensure that the LZW code size does not exceed a maximum allowed value (`gif_lzw_max_code_size`). If the code size is too large, the function returns an error.", "fixing_example": "lzw_cs = gif_get8(s);\nif (lzw_cs > gif_lzw_max_code_size) {\n    sixel_helper_set_additional_message(\n        \"Unsupported GIF (LZW code size)\");\n    status = SIXEL_RUNTIME_ERROR;\n    goto end;\n}\nclear = 1 << lzw_cs;"}
{"pattern_name": "Inconsistent Error Handling", "functionality": "The function handles errors inconsistently, sometimes returning directly and sometimes using a common error handling path.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The function returns directly with `-EOPNOTSUPP` in some error cases, bypassing the common error handling path.", "vulnerable_example": "if (!nfp_abm_u32_check_knode(alink->abm, knode, proto, extack))\n  goto err_delete;\n...\nif ((iter->val & cmask) == (val & cmask) &&\n    iter->band != knode->res->classid) {\n  NL_SET_ERR_MSG_MOD(extack, \"conflict with already offloaded filter\");\n  goto err_delete;\n}", "fixing_pattern": "The fix ensures that all error paths consistently use the `err_delete` label and return the appropriate error code.", "fixing_example": "if (!nfp_abm_u32_check_knode(alink->abm, knode, proto, extack)) {\n  err = -EOPNOTSUPP;\n  goto err_delete;\n}\n...\nif ((iter->val & cmask) == (val & cmask) &&\n    iter->band != knode->res->classid) {\n  NL_SET_ERR_MSG_MOD(extack, \"conflict with already offloaded filter\");\n  err = -EOPNOTSUPP;\n  goto err_delete;\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure Environment Variable Handling", "functionality": "The code handles environment variables without proper validation or fallback mechanisms, potentially leading to insecure configurations.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using environment variables directly without validation or secure fallback mechanisms.", "vulnerable_example": "def get_config():\n    return os.environ['SECRET_KEY']", "fixing_pattern": "Implement secure fallback mechanisms and proper error handling for missing environment variables.", "fixing_example": "def generate_key_if_missing(key):\n    value = env.str(key, \"\")\n    if value == \"\":\n        value = get_random_secret_key()\n        try:\n            with open(env_file, 'a') as f:\n                f.write(f'\\n{key}={value}\\n')\n        except Exception as e:\n            print(f'Warning: failed to write {key} to .env file')\n        os.environ[key] = value\n    return value"}
{"pattern_name": "Incorrect Function Usage in Configuration Handling", "functionality": "The code is responsible for creating or merging server configuration settings. The vulnerable version uses a function intended for creating new configurations instead of merging existing ones.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using a configuration creation function where a configuration merge function is required, potentially leading to incorrect or incomplete configuration handling.", "vulnerable_example": "void *h2_config_create_svr(apr_pool_t *pool, server_rec *s)\n{\n    return h2_config_create(pool, \"srv\", s->defn_name);\n}", "fixing_pattern": "Replacing the configuration creation function with the appropriate configuration merge function to properly handle existing and new configuration settings.", "fixing_example": "void *h2_config_merge_svr(apr_pool_t *pool, void *basev, void *addv)\n{\n    return h2_config_merge(pool, basev, addv);\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Overly Complex Setup with Potential Security Risks", "functionality": "The code sets up a complex environment for testing, including mock objects and configurations for various components like identity, certificates, and DBus interfaces.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The setup method initializes multiple components and mocks, including DBus interfaces, without clear access control mechanisms. This complexity can lead to unintended exposure of sensitive methods or data.", "vulnerable_example": "def setUp(self):\n    self.addCleanup(patch.stopall)\n    cfg_patcher = patch.object(subscription_manager.managercli, 'cfg', new=stubs.config.CFG)\n    self.mock_cfg = cfg_patcher.start()\n    id_mock = NonCallableMock(name='FixtureIdentityMock')\n    id_mock.exists_and_valid = Mock(return_value=True)\n    id_mock.uuid = 'fixture_identity_mock_uuid'\n    id_mock.name = 'fixture_identity_mock_name'\n    id_mock.cert_dir_path = \"/not/a/real/path/to/pki/consumer/\"\n    id_mock.keypath.return_value = \"/not/a/real/key/path\"\n    id_mock.certpath.return_value = \"/not/a/real/cert/path\"\n    self.mock_calc = NonCallableMock()\n    self.mock_calc.calculate.return_value = None\n    self.mock_repofile_path_exists_patcher = patch('subscription_manager.repolib.RepoFile.path_exists')\n    mock_repofile_path_exists = self.mock_repofile_path_exists_patcher.start()\n    mock_repofile_path_exists.return_value = True\n    inj.provide(inj.IDENTITY, id_mock)\n    inj.provide(inj.PRODUCT_DATE_RANGE_CALCULATOR, self.mock_calc)\n    inj.provide(inj.ENTITLEMENT_STATUS_CACHE, stubs.StubEntitlementStatusCache())\n    inj.provide(inj.PROD_STATUS_CACHE, stubs.StubProductStatusCache())\n    inj.provide(inj.OVERRIDE_STATUS_CACHE, stubs.StubOverrideStatusCache())\n    inj.provide(inj.RELEASE_STATUS_CACHE, stubs.StubReleaseStatusCache())\n    inj.provide(inj.PROFILE_MANAGER, stubs.StubProfileManager())\n    self.ent_dir = stubs.StubEntitlementDirectory()\n    inj.provide(inj.ENT_DIR, self.ent_dir)\n    self.prod_dir = stubs.StubProductDirectory()\n    inj.provide(inj.PROD_DIR, self.prod_dir)\n    inj.provide(inj.INSTALLED_PRODUCTS_MANAGER, stubs.StubInstalledProductsManager())\n    self.stub_cp_provider = stubs.StubCPProvider()\n    self._release_versions = []\n    self.stub_cp_provider.content_connection.get_versions = self._get_release_versions\n    inj.provide(inj.CP_PROVIDER, self.stub_cp_provider)\n    inj.provide(inj.CERT_SORTER, stubs.StubCertSorter())\n    plugin_manager_mock = MagicMock(name='FixturePluginManagerMock')\n    plugin_manager_mock.runiter.return_value = iter([])\n    inj.provide(inj.PLUGIN_MANAGER, plugin_manager_mock)\n    inj.provide(inj.DBUS_IFACE, Mock(name='FixtureDbusIfaceMock'))\n    pooltype_cache = Mock()\n    inj.provide(inj.POOLTYPE_CACHE, pooltype_cache)\n    inj.provide(inj.ACTION_LOCK, RLock)\n    self.stub_facts = stubs.StubFacts()\n    inj.provide(inj.FACTS, self.stub_facts)\n    self.dbus_patcher = patch('subscription_manager.managercli.CliCommand._request_validity_check')\n    self.dbus_patcher.start()\n    self.is_valid_server_patcher = patch(\"subscription_manager.managercli.is_valid_server_info\")\n    is_valid_server_mock = self.is_valid_server_patcher.start()\n    is_valid_server_mock.return_value = True\n    self.test_proxy_connection_patcher = patch(\"subscription_manager.managercli.CliCommand.test_proxy_connection\")\n    test_proxy_connection_mock = self.test_proxy_connection_patcher.start()\n    test_proxy_connection_mock.return_value = True\n    self.files_to_cleanup = []", "fixing_pattern": "The fix simplifies the setup by removing the complex initialization of multiple components and mocks, reducing the attack surface and potential security risks.", "fixing_example": "def set_facts(self):\n    return {\"mock.facts\": \"true\"}"}
{"pattern_name": "Hardcoded Credentials in Test Code", "functionality": "The code tests the login functionality of a web application by sending HTTP POST requests with both invalid and valid credentials to a login endpoint. It then checks the response for expected error messages or successful login indicators.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code hardcodes sensitive credentials (usernames and passwords) directly in the source code. This practice is insecure because it exposes credentials to anyone with access to the codebase, making it easier for attackers to exploit them.", "vulnerable_example": "def test_login_functionality():\n    username = 'user'\n    password = 'password'\n    response = requests.post('https://example.com/login', data={'username': username, 'password': password})", "fixing_pattern": "To resolve this issue, credentials should be stored securely using a secrets management system or environment variables. This ensures that sensitive information is not embedded in the code and can be managed securely.", "fixing_example": "import os\n\ndef test_login_functionality():\n    username = os.getenv('TEST_USERNAME')\n    password = os.getenv('TEST_PASSWORD')\n    response = requests.post('https://example.com/login', data={'username': username, 'password': password})"}
{"pattern_name": "Unchecked Function Return Value", "functionality": "The code processes an image and writes it to a dynamic context, then extracts the data from the context.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code does not check the return value of the function `gdImageWBMPCtx`, which could fail and lead to undefined behavior when extracting data from the context.", "vulnerable_example": "gdImageWBMPCtx(im, fg, out);\nrv = gdDPExtractData(out, size);", "fixing_pattern": "The fix adds a check for the return value of `_gdImageWBMPCtx` (likely a corrected version of `gdImageWBMPCtx`). If the function fails, it avoids extracting data from the context and returns NULL instead.", "fixing_example": "if (!_gdImageWBMPCtx(im, fg, out)) {\n    rv = gdDPExtractData(out, size);\n} else {\n    rv = NULL;\n}"}
