{"pattern_name": "Incomplete Response Handling", "functionality": "The code checks for null conditions but does not properly return the response in those cases.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Performing checks without returning the appropriate response, leading to potential null pointer exceptions or incomplete handling.", "vulnerable_example": "if (key == null) {\n    ResponseEntity.notFound();\n}\n...\nif (file == null) {\n    ResponseEntity.notFound();\n}", "fixing_pattern": "Ensuring that all conditional checks return the appropriate response with the correct HTTP status.", "fixing_example": "if (key == null) {\n    return ResponseEntity.notFound().build();\n}\n...\nif (file == null) {\n    return ResponseEntity.notFound().build();\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Unsafe HTML Rendering with `mark_safe`", "functionality": "The code dynamically generates HTML content by concatenating strings and marks the final result as safe for rendering in a web page.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using `mark_safe()` to render HTML content that includes user-controlled data without proper escaping, which can lead to XSS vulnerabilities.", "vulnerable_example": "return mark_safe(template)", "fixing_pattern": "Replacing `mark_safe()` with `format_html()` to safely render HTML content by automatically escaping user-controlled data.", "fixing_example": "return format_html('<a href=\"{}\">{}</a>', peer.get_absolute_url(), peer)"}
