{"pattern_name": "Exposure of Sensitive Information in Configuration", "functionality": "The code handles sensitive information (e.g., passwords) in a configuration object and exposes it directly in the configuration.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code directly assigns and retrieves sensitive information (e.g., passwords) in the configuration object without proper masking or encryption.", "vulnerable_example": "TfsMaterial material = new TfsMaterial(new GoCipher(), new UrlArgument(\"http://url/\"), \"user\", \"domain\", \"password\", \"$project/path/\");\nTfsMaterialConfig config = (TfsMaterialConfig) material.config();\nassertThat(config.getPassword()).isEqualTo(\"password\");\nassertThat(config.getEncryptedPassword()).isNotNull();", "fixing_pattern": "The code now ensures that sensitive information (e.g., passwords) is not exposed in the configuration object by masking or removing it when retrieving attributes.", "fixing_example": "TfsMaterial material = new TfsMaterial(new UrlArgument(\"http://username:password@tfsrepo.com\"), \"username\", \"domain\", \"password\", \"$project/path/\");\nMap<String, Object> attributes = material.getAttributes(false);\nassertThat(attributes.get(\"type\")).isEqualTo(\"tfs\");\nMap<String, Object> configuration = (Map<String, Object>) attributes.get(\"tfs-configuration\");\nassertThat(configuration.get(\"url\")).isEqualTo(\"http://username:******@tfsrepo.com\");\nassertThat(configuration.get(\"domain\")).isEqualTo(\"domain\");\nassertThat(configuration.get(\"username\")).isEqualTo(\"username\");\nassertThat(configuration.get(\"password\")).isNull();\nassertThat(configuration.get(\"project-path\")).isEqualTo(\"$project/path/\");"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure API Key Handling", "functionality": "The code sets a secure cookie with an API key if the user credentials are valid.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code initially stores the API key in a variable, which could be exposed through command line history or process information.", "vulnerable_example": "api_key = sickbeard.API_KEY", "fixing_pattern": "The fix removes the explicit storage of the API key in a variable and directly uses the stored API key to set the secure cookie.", "fixing_example": "self.set_secure_cookie('sickrage_user', sickbeard.API_KEY, expires_days=remember_me)"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure Authentication Bypass via Remember-Me Cookie", "functionality": "The system uses a remember-me cookie to authenticate users without requiring them to provide credentials on subsequent visits. The system assumes proper authentication if a remember-me cookie is present, even if the cookie is incorrect, especially when the endpoint allows anonymous access.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The system does not properly validate the remember-me cookie against the user's actual authentication state, allowing an attacker to forge a cookie and assume the identity of another user.", "vulnerable_example": "if (rememberMeCookie != null) {\n    authenticateUser(rememberMeCookie.getUsername());\n}", "fixing_pattern": "The system now properly validates the remember-me cookie by checking its authenticity and ensuring it matches the user's actual authentication state before granting access.", "fixing_example": "if (rememberMeCookie != null && isValidRememberMeCookie(rememberMeCookie)) {\n    authenticateUser(rememberMeCookie.getUsername());\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure Configuration Initialization", "functionality": "The code initializes a configuration object for handling system settings and authentication.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Directly initializing configuration using a module-level function without proper access control checks.", "vulnerable_example": "self.rhsmcfg = rhsm.config.initConfig()", "fixing_pattern": "Wrapping the configuration initialization in a controlled class instance to enforce access control.", "fixing_example": "self.rhsmcfg = config.Config(initConfig())"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure File Handling with Missing Existence Check", "functionality": "The code attempts to load and modify a configuration file without first verifying its existence.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Attempting to load a file without checking if it exists, which can lead to undefined behavior or crashes if the file is missing.", "vulnerable_example": "keyfile = g_key_file_new();\nif (!g_key_file_load_from_file(keyfile, conf_path, G_KEY_FILE_KEEP_COMMENTS, error))\n    return FALSE;", "fixing_pattern": "Adding a file existence check before attempting to load the file, ensuring the file is present and accessible.", "fixing_example": "if (!g_file_test(conf_path, G_FILE_TEST_EXISTS)) {\n    g_set_error(error, FWUPD_ERROR, FWUPD_ERROR_NOT_FOUND, \"%s is missing\", conf_path);\n    return FALSE;\n}\nif (!g_key_file_load_from_file(keyfile, conf_path, G_KEY_FILE_KEEP_COMMENTS, error))\n    return FALSE;"}
{"pattern_name": "Unsafe Context Object Usage", "functionality": "The code uses a context object (h2_ctx) to retrieve server configuration settings, which can lead to misconfiguration if the context is not properly initialized or synchronized.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using a context object (h2_ctx) to retrieve server configuration settings, which can lead to misconfiguration if the context is not properly initialized or synchronized.", "vulnerable_example": "session->s = h2_ctx_server_get(ctx);\nsession->config = h2_config_sget(session->s);\nsession->max_stream_count = h2_config_geti(session->config, H2_CONF_MAX_STREAMS);", "fixing_pattern": "Directly passing the server configuration settings as a parameter to the function, ensuring that the configuration is consistent and properly initialized.", "fixing_example": "session->s = s;\nsession->max_stream_count = h2_config_sgeti(s, H2_CONF_MAX_STREAMS);"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure Jinja2 Environment Configuration", "functionality": "The code configures a Jinja2 environment without enabling autoescaping, which can lead to security vulnerabilities such as XSS.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Creating a Jinja2 environment without enabling autoescaping.", "vulnerable_example": "The code initializes a Jinja2 environment without the `autoescape` parameter, leaving it vulnerable to XSS attacks.", "fixing_pattern": "Enabling autoescaping in the Jinja2 environment to prevent XSS attacks.", "fixing_example": "The code now includes `autoescape=select_autoescape()` when creating the Jinja2 environment, ensuring that autoescaping is enabled to mitigate XSS risks."}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure Parameter Handling in Test Cases", "functionality": "The test cases handle parameters that could be manipulated to execute arbitrary code.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The test cases include parameters that are not properly sanitized, making them susceptible to injection attacks.", "vulnerable_example": "assertPlotParam(\"smooth\", \"unwrap\");", "fixing_pattern": "The fix involves updating the test cases to handle parameters securely by adding validation and sanitization checks.", "fixing_example": "assertInvalidPlotParam(\"fgcolor\", \"%5BDEADBE\");"}
{"pattern_name": "Insufficient Capability Check", "functionality": "The code checks if a process has the necessary capabilities to perform certain operations, such as changing user IDs or group IDs, or performing administrative tasks.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code uses `nsown_capable(CAP_SYS_ADMIN)` to check if the current process has the `CAP_SYS_ADMIN` capability in its own user namespace. This does not account for the possibility that the process might be in a different user namespace, potentially allowing unauthorized access.", "vulnerable_example": "if ((creds->pid == task_tgid_vnr(current) || nsown_capable(CAP_SYS_ADMIN)) && ...)", "fixing_pattern": "The fix replaces `nsown_capable(CAP_SYS_ADMIN)` with `ns_capable(current->nsproxy->pid_ns->user_ns, CAP_SYS_ADMIN)`, which checks the capability in the correct user namespace, ensuring that the process has the necessary permissions in the context of the target namespace.", "fixing_example": "if ((creds->pid == task_tgid_vnr(current) || ns_capable(current->nsproxy->pid_ns->user_ns, CAP_SYS_ADMIN)) && ...)"}
{"pattern_name": "Resource Leak on Error Path", "functionality": "The code attempts to bind ports and start a connection. If either operation fails, it enters a retry loop with an increasing delay.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code does not properly clean up resources (unbind ports) before entering the retry loop when an error occurs.", "vulnerable_example": "if(bind_ports() | exec_connect_start()) {\n    s_poll_sleep(delay/1000, delay%1000);\n    signal_post(SIGNAL_RELOAD_CONFIG);\n    delay*=2;\n    if(delay > 10000)\n        delay=10000;\n} else {\n    delay=10;\n}", "fixing_pattern": "Added resource cleanup (unbind_ports) before entering the retry loop to ensure proper resource management on error paths.", "fixing_example": "if(bind_ports() | exec_connect_start()) {\n    unbind_ports();\n    s_poll_sleep(delay/1000, delay%1000);\n    signal_post(SIGNAL_RELOAD_CONFIG);\n    delay*=2;\n    if(delay > 10000)\n        delay=10000;\n} else {\n    delay=10;\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Inefficient Authentication Check on Main Event Loop", "functionality": "The code checks user permissions for accessing an API endpoint by iterating through the required permissions and verifying authorization for each one. This check is performed synchronously on the main event loop.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The authorization check is performed on the main event loop, which can block other requests and lead to a denial of service if the check is computationally expensive or if there are many invalid requests.", "vulnerable_example": "if (authenticationService.isPresent()) {\n  if (optionalUser.isPresent()) {\n    User user = optionalUser.get();\n    for (String perm : jsonRpcMethod.getPermissions()) {\n      user.isAuthorized(\n          perm,\n          (authed) -> {\n            if (authed.result()) {\n              foundMatchingPermission.set(true);\n            }\n          });\n    }\n  }\n}", "fixing_pattern": "The fix optimizes the permission check by returning early if a matching permission is found, reducing unnecessary iterations. Additionally, it simplifies the logic by handling the case where no authentication service is present separately.", "fixing_example": "if (authenticationService.isEmpty()) {\n  return true;\n}\nif (optionalUser.isPresent()) {\n  User user = optionalUser.get();\n  for (String perm : jsonRpcMethod.getPermissions()) {\n    user.isAuthorized(\n        perm,\n        (authed) -> {\n          if (authed.result()) {\n            foundMatchingPermission.set(true);\n          }\n        });\n    if (foundMatchingPermission.get()) {\n      return foundMatchingPermission.get();\n    }\n  }\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Improper Path Validation", "functionality": "The code copies a source folder to a destination folder, performs code signing on an application, and builds a package using command-line tools.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code dynamically constructs file paths using user-supplied input without proper validation. This can lead to path traversal or symlink attacks if the input is maliciously crafted.", "vulnerable_example": "const srcPath = path.join(src, userInput);\nif (fs.existsSync(srcPath)) {\n    fs.copyFileSync(srcPath, destPath);\n}", "fixing_pattern": "Validate and sanitize user-supplied input to ensure it does not contain malicious characters or sequences. Use absolute paths and restrict access to sensitive directories.", "fixing_example": "const srcPath = path.resolve(src, userInput);\nif (srcPath.startsWith('/safe/directory/') && fs.existsSync(srcPath)) {\n    fs.copyFileSync(srcPath, destPath);\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Hardcoded Set of Values", "functionality": "The code uses a hardcoded set of supported file formats.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using a hardcoded set of values that may not be easily maintainable or extensible.", "vulnerable_example": "EXTENSIONS_READER = {'TXT', 'PDF', 'EPUB', 'CBZ', 'CBT', 'CBR', 'DJVU'}", "fixing_pattern": "Using a variable with a more descriptive name and ensuring it is in lowercase to match the case-insensitive comparison.", "fixing_example": "extensions_reader = {'TXT', 'PDF', 'EPUB', 'CBZ', 'CBT', 'CBR', 'DJVU'}"}
{"pattern_name": "Hardcoded API Key", "functionality": "The code defines constants for various API keys and URLs used in the application, including a Google Maps API key, to facilitate integration with external services.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The vulnerability arises from hardcoding sensitive information, such as API keys, directly in the source code. This practice exposes the keys to anyone with access to the codebase, making it easier for attackers to misuse them.", "vulnerable_example": "define('LL_STRIPE_KEY', 'sk_test_51JkLZ2SJmQ9WVdrOVREWTJXVnBFTXpBbWNHbzlNQS0tJnM9Y29uc3VtZXJzZWNyZXQmeD02Mw');", "fixing_pattern": "To resolve this issue, sensitive information like API keys should be stored in environment variables or secure configuration files outside the codebase. This ensures that the keys are not exposed in the source code and can be managed securely.", "fixing_example": "define('LL_GMAPS_KEY', getenv('GMAPS_API_KEY'));"}
{"pattern_name": "Missing Token Expiration Validation", "functionality": "The code handles authentication and token generation for users, including creating and storing tokens in the token API.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The token creation process does not include the token's expiration time when storing it in the token API, which could lead to tokens being valid indefinitely or beyond their intended lifespan.", "vulnerable_example": "self.token_api.create_token(\n    context, token_id, dict(key=token_id,\n                            id=token_id,\n                            user=user_ref,\n                            tenant=tenant_ref,\n                            metadata=metadata_ref))", "fixing_pattern": "The fix adds the token's expiration time to the token data when storing it in the token API, ensuring that the token's validity period is properly enforced.", "fixing_example": "self.token_api.create_token(\n    context, token_id, dict(key=token_id,\n                            id=token_id,\n                            expires=auth_token_data['expires'],\n                            user=user_ref,\n                            tenant=tenant_ref,\n                            metadata=metadata_ref))"}
{"pattern_name": "Hardcoded Credentials", "functionality": "The code initializes data by hashing passwords and storing them in a Redis database.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code contains hardcoded passwords ('adminpass' and 'testpass'), which are stored directly in the source code. This practice exposes sensitive credentials to unauthorized access, especially if the code is shared or leaked.", "vulnerable_example": "admin_password = 'adminpass'\ntest_password = 'testpass'", "fixing_pattern": "Remove hardcoded credentials and use a secure key/secret management system (e.g., environment variables, vaults, or configuration files with restricted access) to store and retrieve sensitive information.", "fixing_example": "import os\n\nadmin_password = os.getenv('ADMIN_PASSWORD')\ntest_password = os.getenv('TEST_PASSWORD')"}
{"pattern_name": "Missing Authorization in Database Operations", "functionality": "The code connects to a SQLite database, creates a table with specified columns and a primary key, and queries the database based on provided conditions. It returns the results of the query.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code lacks proper authorization checks before executing critical database operations, such as creating a table or querying data. This allows unauthorized users to perform potentially harmful actions on the database.", "vulnerable_example": "<?php\nfunction createAndQueryTable($dbname, $table, $columns, $primaryKey, $conditions) {\n    $db = new PDO(\"sqlite:$dbname\");\n    $db->setAttribute(PDO::ATTR_ERRMODE, PDO::ERRMODE_EXCEPTION);\n    $columnDefinitions = implode(\", \", $columns);\n    $sql = \"CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS $table ($columnDefinitions, PRIMARY KEY ($primaryKey))\";\n    $db->exec($sql); // Vulnerable line\n    $query = \"SELECT * FROM $table WHERE $conditions\";\n    $stmt = $db->prepare($query);\n    $stmt->execute();\n    return $stmt->fetchAll(PDO::FETCH_ASSOC);\n}\n?>", "fixing_pattern": "Implement authorization checks to ensure that only authorized users can perform critical database operations. This can be done by validating user permissions before executing the database query or table creation.", "fixing_example": "<?php\nfunction createAndQueryTable($dbname, $table, $columns, $primaryKey, $conditions, $userRole) {\n    if ($userRole !== 'admin') {\n        throw new Exception(\"Unauthorized access\");\n    }\n    $db = new PDO(\"sqlite:$dbname\");\n    $db->setAttribute(PDO::ATTR_ERRMODE, PDO::ERRMODE_EXCEPTION);\n    $columnDefinitions = implode(\", \", $columns);\n    $sql = \"CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS $table ($columnDefinitions, PRIMARY KEY ($primaryKey))\";\n    $db->exec($sql); // Now protected by authorization check\n    $query = \"SELECT * FROM $table WHERE $conditions\";\n    $stmt = $db->prepare($query);\n    $stmt->execute();\n    return $stmt->fetchAll(PDO::FETCH_ASSOC);\n}\n?>"}
{"pattern_name": "Unrestricted Model Field Access", "functionality": "The code allows access to model fields without proper validation of whether the field represents a relationship between models.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code does not validate if a field is a relationship field before allowing access to it, potentially exposing sensitive information.", "vulnerable_example": "def get_field_value(model, field_name):\n    return getattr(model, field_name)", "fixing_pattern": "The fix involves adding validation to ensure that the field is a relationship field before allowing access to it.", "fixing_example": "def get_field_value(model, field_name):\n    field = model._meta.get_field(field_name)\n    if not field.is_relation:\n        raise ValueError('Field is not a relationship field')\n    return getattr(model, field_name)"}
{"pattern_name": "Insufficient Authorization Scope Validation", "functionality": "The code defines an HTTP POST endpoint for creating a purchase order. It validates the input data, checks for the existence of a store and wallet, creates a purchase order object, and saves it to the database.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The `[Authorization]` attribute specifies a scope (`purchase-orders.edit`), but there is no explicit validation to ensure the user has the required permissions to perform the action. This could allow unauthorized users to exploit the endpoint if other security mechanisms are bypassed.", "vulnerable_example": "[HttpPost, Route(\"orders/create\"), Authorization(Scope = \"orders.edit\")]\npublic async Task<ActionResult> CreateOrder(OrderViewModel model)\n{\n    // Logic to create an order\n}", "fixing_pattern": "Explicitly validate the user's permissions within the method logic, in addition to the `[Authorization]` attribute. This ensures that only users with the appropriate permissions can perform the action.", "fixing_example": "[HttpPost, Route(\"purchase-orders/create\"), Authorization(Scope = \"purchase-orders.edit\")]\n[ValidateAntiForgeryToken]\npublic async Task<ActionResult> Create(CreatePurchaseOrderViewModel viewModel)\n{\n    if (!User.HasPermission(\"purchase-orders.edit\"))\n    {\n        return this.Error(\"Insufficient permissions.\");\n    }\n    // Logic to create a purchase order\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Insufficient Input Validation in System Control Handler", "functionality": "The code handles system control table entries by processing user-provided input values without proper validation.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using a generic input processing function (proc_dointvec) that does not enforce value constraints, allowing potentially dangerous values to be set.", "vulnerable_example": "int ret = proc_dointvec(table, write, buffer, lenp, ppos);", "fixing_pattern": "Replacing the generic input processing function with a constrained version (proc_dointvec_minmax) that enforces minimum and maximum value limits.", "fixing_example": "int ret = proc_dointvec_minmax(table, write, buffer, lenp, ppos);"}
{"pattern_name": "Missing CSRF Protection in HTTP POST Method", "functionality": "The code defines a generic controller that handles HTTP requests for a given model type. It includes methods for retrieving, adding, and updating model instances using a repository interface.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The `[HttpPost]` method handler lacks the `[ValidateAntiForgeryToken]` attribute, which is necessary to protect against Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) attacks. Without this attribute, the application cannot verify that the request originated from an authorized user, making it vulnerable to unauthorized state-changing requests.", "vulnerable_example": "[HttpPost]\npublic ActionResult<T> Post([FromBody] T model)\n{\n    if (model == null)\n    {\n        return BadRequest();\n    }\n\n    repository.Add(model);\n    return CreatedAtAction(nameof(Get), new { id = (model as dynamic).Id }, model);\n}", "fixing_pattern": "To resolve the vulnerability, the `[ValidateAntiForgeryToken]` attribute should be added to the `[HttpPost]` method. This ensures that the application validates the anti-forgery token included in the request, confirming that the request is legitimate and originated from an authorized user.", "fixing_example": "[HttpPost]\n[ValidateAntiForgeryToken]\npublic ActionResult<T> Post([FromBody] T model)\n{\n    if (model == null)\n    {\n        return BadRequest();\n    }\n\n    repository.Add(model);\n    return CreatedAtAction(nameof(Get), new { id = (model as dynamic).Id }, model);\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Missing CSRF Protection in HTTP POST Handler", "functionality": "The code handles user registration by accepting a POST request with user registration data, validating the input, checking for existing users, and adding a new user to the repository.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The HTTP POST handler lacks the `[ValidateAntiForgeryToken]` attribute, making it vulnerable to Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) attacks. Without this attribute, the application cannot verify that the request originated from an authenticated user, allowing malicious actors to forge requests and perform unauthorized actions.", "vulnerable_example": "[HttpPost]\npublic IActionResult ChangePassword([FromBody] ChangePasswordModel model)\n{\n    if (model == null)\n    {\n        return BadRequest(\"Invalid password data.\");\n    }\n\n    userRepository.ChangePassword(model);\n    return Ok(\"Password changed successfully.\");\n}", "fixing_pattern": "To mitigate CSRF vulnerabilities, the `[ValidateAntiForgeryToken]` attribute should be added to the HTTP POST handler. This ensures that the request includes a valid anti-forgery token, verifying its authenticity and preventing unauthorized requests.", "fixing_example": "[HttpPost]\n[ValidateAntiForgeryToken]\npublic IActionResult RegisterUser([FromBody] UserRegistrationModel userRegistration)\n{\n    if (userRegistration == null)\n    {\n        return BadRequest(\"Invalid user registration data.\");\n    }\n\n    if (!ModelState.IsValid)\n    {\n        return BadRequest(ModelState);\n    }\n\n    if (userRepository.UserExists(userRegistration.Username))\n    {\n        return BadRequest(\"Username already exists.\");\n    }\n\n    if (!userRepository.IsEmailValid(userRegistration.Email))\n    {\n        return BadRequest(\"Invalid email address.\");\n    }\n\n    var user = new User\n    {\n        Username = userRegistration.Username,\n        Email = userRegistration.Email,\n        PasswordHash = HashPassword(userRegistration.Password)\n    };\n\n    userRepository.AddUser(user);\n    return Ok(\"User registered successfully.\");\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Unrestricted HTTP Request to Metadata Services", "functionality": "The code sends an HTTP POST request to a specified URI with form data and authentication credentials.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The vulnerable code does not restrict or validate the URI, allowing requests to potentially sensitive metadata service endpoints.", "vulnerable_example": "protected PostMethod executePostForm(String uri, NameValuePair[] nameValuePairs, String userName, String password)\n    throws Exception\n{\n    HttpClient httpClient = new HttpClient();\n    httpClient.getState().setCredentials(AuthScope.ANY, new UsernamePasswordCredentials(userName, password));\n    httpClient.getParams().setAuthenticationPreemptive(true);\n    PostMethod postMethod = new PostMethod(uri);\n    postMethod.addRequestHeader(\"Accept\", MediaType.APPLICATION_XML.toString());\n    postMethod.addRequestHeader(\"Content-type\", MediaType.APPLICATION_WWW_FORM.toString());\n    postMethod.setRequestBody(nameValuePairs);\n    httpClient.executeMethod(postMethod);\n    return postMethod;\n}", "fixing_pattern": "The fix introduces additional validation and security measures by adding a form token header and changing the request body handling, but the core issue of URI validation is not explicitly addressed in the provided commit.", "fixing_example": "protected PostMethod executePost(String uri, String string, String mediaType, String userName, String password,\n    String formToken) throws Exception\n{\n    HttpClient httpClient = new HttpClient();\n    httpClient.getState().setCredentials(AuthScope.ANY, new UsernamePasswordCredentials(userName, password));\n    httpClient.getParams().setAuthenticationPreemptive(true);\n    PostMethod postMethod = new PostMethod(uri);\n    postMethod.addRequestHeader(\"Accept\", MediaType.APPLICATION_XML.toString());\n    if (formToken != null) {\n        postMethod.addRequestHeader(\"XWiki-Form-Token\", formToken);\n    }\n    RequestEntity entity = new StringRequestEntity(string, mediaType, \"UTF-8\");\n    postMethod.setRequestEntity(entity);\n    httpClient.executeMethod(postMethod);\n    return postMethod;\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Direct SQL Query Construction Without Validation", "functionality": "The code retrieves a list of objects from a database based on a collection of UIDs (unique identifiers) by directly passing the UIDs to a store method without validating or sanitizing them.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code directly passes user-supplied UIDs to a database query method (`store.getByUid(uids)`), which can lead to SQL injection if the UIDs are not properly sanitized.", "vulnerable_example": "return (List<T>) store.getByUid( uids );", "fixing_pattern": "The code now retrieves the objects using a safe method (`getByUid`) and then validates the retrieved UIDs against the input UIDs. If there is a mismatch, it throws an `IllegalQueryException`, ensuring that only valid and existing UIDs are processed.", "fixing_example": "List<T> objects = getByUid( type, uids );\nList<String> identifiers = IdentifiableObjectUtils.getUids( objects );\nList<String> difference = CollectionUtils.difference( uids, identifiers );\nif ( !difference.isEmpty() )\n{\n    throw new IllegalQueryException( new ErrorMessage(\n        ErrorCode.E1112, type.getSimpleName(), difference ) );\n}\nreturn objects;"}
{"pattern_name": "Matrix Variable Path Bypass", "functionality": "The code processes HTTP requests and routes them to the appropriate service handlers. It uses the request path to determine the correct handler, including paths that may contain matrix variables.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code does not validate or properly handle matrix variables in the request path, which can lead to incorrect routing and bypassing of security decorators.", "vulnerable_example": "The original code does not include any validation or handling for matrix variables in the request path, which can be exploited to bypass security checks.", "fixing_pattern": "The fix introduces validation of the request path to ensure that matrix variables are properly handled. If the path contains invalid matrix variables, the request is rejected with a BAD_REQUEST response.", "fixing_example": "final String mappedPath = mappedPath(ctx);\nif (mappedPath == null) {\n    return HttpResponse.of(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST, MediaType.PLAIN_TEXT_UTF_8,\n                           \"Invalid matrix variable: \" +\n                           ctx.routingContext().requestTarget().maybePathWithMatrixVariables());\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure Configuration Handling", "functionality": "The code handles configuration settings for server and proxy connections, including insecure settings.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Directly setting insecure configuration values without proper validation or access control.", "vulnerable_example": "cfg.set(\"server\", \"insecure\", \"1\")", "fixing_pattern": "Using a more secure configuration handling mechanism that ensures proper validation and access control.", "fixing_example": "conf[\"server\"][\"insecure\"] = \"1\""}
{"pattern_name": "Lack of Input Restriction in Security Context", "functionality": "The code processes user input in a security-sensitive context without enforcing restrictions or checks to prevent unauthorized access to resources.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code processes user input (`full` or `code`) in a security-sensitive context (e.g., accessing internal resources) without enforcing restrictions or checks to prevent unauthorized access.", "vulnerable_example": "String full = arg.get(\"FULL\", 0);\nif (full == null) {\n    full = code;\n}", "fixing_pattern": "The fix enforces restrictions or checks on user input to ensure it adheres to security policies, preventing unauthorized access to resources.", "fixing_example": "String full = arg.get(\"FULL\", 0);\nif (full == null) {\n    full = code;\n}\n// Enforce restrictions or checks on `full` to prevent unauthorized access."}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure Password Handling in Command Line", "functionality": "The code handles user authentication by comparing input credentials with stored credentials and sets a secure cookie if the credentials match.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code initially stores the password in a variable, which could be exposed through command line history or process information.", "vulnerable_example": "password = sickbeard.WEB_PASSWORD", "fixing_pattern": "The fix removes the explicit storage of the password in a variable and directly compares the input with the stored password.", "fixing_example": "if self.get_argument('username', '') == sickbeard.WEB_USERNAME and self.get_argument('password', '') == sickbeard.WEB_PASSWORD:"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure XML External Entity (XXE) Processing", "functionality": "The code loads and updates a configuration file stored in a secure directory. It reads the file using an XML parser, applies updates to the XML document, and saves the changes atomically.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The XML parser is configured to prohibit DTD processing and disable the XML resolver, but the `XmlReader` settings are not explicitly enforced during the `Load` operation. This could allow an attacker to exploit XML External Entity (XXE) processing if the parser inadvertently processes maliciously crafted XML input.", "vulnerable_example": "XmlDocument configurationFile = new XmlDocument();\nusing (XmlReader reader = XmlReader.Create(configFilePath))\n{\n    configurationFile.Load(reader);\n}", "fixing_pattern": "To prevent XXE vulnerabilities, ensure the `XmlReader` settings are explicitly enforced during the `Load` operation. Use a secure `XmlReader` instance with DTD processing prohibited and the XML resolver disabled.", "fixing_example": "XmlDocument configurationFile = new XmlDocument();\nXmlReaderSettings settings = new XmlReaderSettings\n{\n    DtdProcessing = DtdProcessing.Prohibit,\n    XmlResolver = null\n};\nusing (XmlReader reader = XmlReader.Create(configFilePath, settings))\n{\n    configurationFile.Load(reader);\n}"}
